{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1787919597695,"sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","part":{"id":"prt_04850007e001aH0QQwdNL0utVi","messageID":"msg_0484ffc03001UObwaP6Ap88UZ0","sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787919598197,"sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_00_ET_UBVMjndcPOheX0GTpm8W3809","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/internal/launch/launch.go"},"output":"<path>/workspace/src/internal/launch/launch.go</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: // Package launch spawns manigot agent sessions in a new terminal window or\n2: // pane so the TUI can keep running while the agent works.\n3: //\n4: // Spawn order (macOS + Linux; no Windows v1):\n5: //  1. Inside tmux — when the TUI is itself running inside tmux (detected via\n6: //     the $TMUX env var, with a tmux binary on PATH), this works the same on\n7: //     every platform. The session is opened as a split pane in the TUI's\n8: //     current window (`tmux split-window), replacing the pane manigot opened\n9: //     last so at most one manigot pane exists at a time (the replace policy).\n10: //     Inside tmux this branch always wins — even when\n11: //     config.Settings.Terminal is set — so a session launched from a TUI\n12: //     that is itself inside tmux lands in a tmux pane, never in a separate\n13: //     window.\n14: //  2. config.Settings.Terminal, when set: the explicit user-chosen terminal\n15: //     (e.g. \"kitty\") is invoked directly — see buildOverrideCmd. It applies\n16: //     only when the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is not available there);\n17: //     inside tmux the split pane above wins.\n18: //  3. macOS Terminal.app via osascript.\n19: //  4. A Linux terminal emulator, tried in order: gnome-terminal, ptyxis\n20: //     (Fedora's default GNOME terminal since Fedora 41), x-terminal-emulator\n21: //     (Debian), konsole, xterm.\n22: //\n23: // The command opened is always `<mg-binary> --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` run\n24: // from the project root, matching the session launcher's invocation contract.\n25: // <mg-binary> is the running binary's own path (os.Executable) — the one Go\n26: // binary IS the command now — so an install under a different name still\n27: // works.\n28: package launch\n29: \n30: import (\n31: \t\"errors\"\n32: \t\"fmt\"\n33: \t\"io\"\n34: \t\"os\"\n35: \t\"os/exec\"\n36: \t\"runtime\"\n37: \t\"strings\"\n38: \t\"sync\"\n39: \n40: \t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/config\"\n41: )\n42: \n43: // Agent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n44: // window (see launchTmuxPane) — that runs manigot with `--profile <profile>\n45: // --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` in projectRoot. profile is one of the\n46: // subscription profiles in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to\n47: // config.ProfileClaudePro, matching the session launcher's own default. terminal is\n48: // config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty and the TUI is NOT inside tmux, that\n49: // terminal is invoked directly instead of the auto-detect chain below — see\n50: // buildOverrideCmd; inside tmux the split pane wins regardless of this setting.\n51: // Empty falls back to the configured override itself (TerminalSetting), so\n52: // callers that don't have the setting loaded — CLI paths like `mg done`'s\n53: // git-solver handoff — still honor it; only when the setting is also unset does\n54: // the auto-detect chain apply. It returns a short\n55: // human description of where it opened (e.g. \"tmux pane\", \"Terminal.app\",\n56: // \"gnome-terminal\", or the override's own binary name) so the caller can surface\n57: // it in a status line.\n58: //\n59: // The launcher runs from projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job\n60: // lives in its own worktree: the session launcher\n61: // (session.ResolveRootFrom) re-derives the effective mount root itself from\n62: // the --job it is given (branch-match + worktree lookup, hard error on a\n63: // worktree-less job), so launching from projectRoot is not just sufficient\n64: // but required — it locates the project from $PWD and only then resolves the\n65: // job's worktree. The same reasoning keeps Quick/AgentQuick (no --job at all, mount\n66: // PROJECT_ROOT directly) and Jdi (mg-jdi, which re-derives per invocation)\n67: // on projectRoot.\n68: //\n69: // The launcher process is detached: its stdio is discarded so it cannot\n70: // corrupt the TUI's alt screen, and it is reaped asynchronously. The one\n71: // exception is the tmux path, where the split-window client is run\n72: // synchronously (see launchTmuxPane) so its pane-id output can be captured\n73: // for the replace policy — split-window itself returns in milliseconds, it\n74: // does not wait for the agent session.\n75: //\n76: // cmd.Start() failing (the launcher binary itself couldn't be\n77: // spawned — e.g. permission denied) is already surfaced: it's returned as an\n78: // error here and shown by the caller (App.updateDetail via cmdErrorText). The\n79: // remaining gap is a launcher that starts successfully but then fails on its\n80: // own after Agent has already returned \"success\" to the caller — e.g.\n81: // gnome-terminal/konsole exiting non-zero because there's no display server.\n82: // Surfacing that would need either blocking on cmd.Wait() here or wiring the\n83: // reaping goroutine back into a tea.Msg, and neither is safe to add: xterm\n84: // (unlike gnome-terminal/konsole/tmux/osascript, which detach quickly) *is*\n85: // the window process, so it doesn't return until the window closes — for it,\n86: // Wait()-ing here would block Update() for the whole agent session, which is\n87: // strictly worse than the bug being fixed. holdOnFailure\n88: // already covers the failure mode the brief actually reports (the inner `sc\n89: // --agent` command failing fast); a launcher-binary-itself failure is a\n90: // narrower, rarer case left uncovered rather than risk reintroducing UI lag.\n91: func Agent(agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n92: \texe, err := ExeOverride()\n93: \tif err != nil {\n94: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n95: \t}\n96: \tinner := shellCommand(exe, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile)\n97: \treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n98: }\n99: \n100: // Quick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n101: // window, exactly like Agent, including the replace policy — that runs manigot\n102: // with just `--profile <profile>` (no --agent, no --job) in projectRoot: a\n103: // bare session for an ad-hoc change that doesn't belong to any specific\n104: // job/agent workflow. profile is one of the subscription profiles in\n105: // config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n106: // matching Agent. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to\n107: // launchDetached exactly like Agent's own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n108: // doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n109: // of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n110: //\n111: // The session launcher treats --agent and --job as optional, so a bare `sc\n112: // --profile <profile>` simply runs claude/opencode against the current project\n113: // with no agent flag and no job prompt.\n114: func Quick(projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n115: \texe, err := ExeOverride()\n116: \tif err != nil {\n117: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n118: \t}\n119: \tinner := quickShellCommand(exe, projectRoot, profile)\n120: \treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n121: }\n122: \n123: // AgentQuick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n124: // current window, exactly like Agent/Quick, including the replace policy —\n125: // that runs manigot with `--profile <profile> --agent <agent>` (no --job) in\n126: // projectRoot: launching a specific agent for an ad-hoc session that doesn't\n127: // belong to any particular job. profile is one of the subscription profiles\n128: // in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n129: // matching Agent/Quick. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded\n130: // through to launchDetached exactly like Agent/Quick's own terminal\n131: // parameter — see Agent's doc for the override semantics. It returns the\n132: // same short human description of where it opened so the caller can surface\n133: // it in a status line.\n134: //\n135: // The session launcher treats --job as optional (see Quick's own doc for the\n136: // --agent/--job-less case), so `sc --profile <profile> --agent <agent>`\n137: // simply runs that agent against the current project with no job prompt.\n138: func AgentQuick(agent, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n139: \texe, err := ExeOverride()\n140: \tif err != nil {\n141: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n142: \t}\n143: \tinner := agentQuickShellCommand(exe, agent, projectRoot, profile)\n144: \treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n145: }\n146: \n147: // HostAgent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n148: // current window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace\n149: // policy — that runs `mg host --profile <profile> --agent <agent> --prompt\n150: // '<prompt>'` in projectRoot: a host-mode agent session (the agent CLI runs\n151: // directly on the host, unisolated — see `mg host`) with an explicit prompt.\n152: // prompt is the free-form instruction the agent starts with (the session\n153: // launcher passes it through verbatim); it is what `mg done` uses to hand a\n154: // failed squash merge to @git-solver, telling it what broke and what cleanup\n155: // remains. profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles;\n156: // an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, matching Agent/Quick.\n157: // terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached\n158: // exactly like the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n159: // doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n160: // of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n161: //\n162: // Unlike Agent/AgentQuick there is no --job: host-mode sessions run against\n163: // the project root itself — the main worktree, where a failed `mg done`\n164: // squash merge leaves its conflicted state — never a job worktree.\n165: func HostAgent(agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n166: \texe, err := ExeOverride()\n167: \tif err != nil {\n168: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n169: \t}\n170: \tinner := hostAgentShellCommand(exe, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile)\n171: \treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n172: }\n173: \n174: // Tig opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n175: // window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace policy —\n176: // that runs `mg diff '<jobName>' --tig` in projectRoot: the host-side tig TUI\n177: // browsing what the job's branch changed (the CLI path already exists —\n178: // cmd/mg/diff.go runTig resolves the job's branch + the project's base\n179: // branch, builds the three-dot range <base>...<branch>, runs `tig <range>`,\n180: // and errors clearly when tig is not installed). terminal is\n181: // config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached exactly like\n182: // the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's doc for the\n183: // override semantics. It returns the same short human description of where it\n184: // opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n185: //\n186: // Unlike Agent/Quick there is no profile flag: `mg diff` is a plain host git\n187: // command, not a session launch, so it takes no --profile (and no --agent or\n188: // --job either).\n189: //\n190: // The job is passed by its Name (id_slug), not its ID: `mg diff` resolves the\n191: // job's branch via an exact match on the branch's id_slug tail segment first,\n192: // then a unique prefix (cmd/mg/diff.go resolveJobBranch) — and job.Name\n193: // equals that tail segment exactly, so resolution is unambiguous.\n194: //\n195: // Tig re-checks availability itself (TigAvailable) before spawning: the\n196: // TUI's cached availability gate (detailView.tigAvailable) is only as fresh\n197: // as the last job open, so a stale cached \"available\" must surface a\n198: // synchronous error here rather than opening a doomed pane that dies on the\n199: // CLI's own tig-missing check.\n200: func Tig(jobID, projectRoot, terminal string) (string, error) {\n201: \tif !TigAvailable() {\n202: \t\treturn \"\", errors.New(\"tig is not installed on the host — install it, or use the diff tab\")\n203: \t}\n204: \texe, err := ExeOverride()\n205: \tif err != nil {\n206: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n207: \t}\n208: \tinner := tigShellCommand(exe, jobID, projectRoot)\n209: \treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n210: }\n211: \n212: // Tail opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n213: // window, exactly like Agent/Quick/Tig, including the replace policy — that\n214: // runs `tail -f '<logPath>'`: a live tail of the given absolute log file (the\n215: // mg-jdi run.log sidecar, job.JDIRunLogPath) so a user can watch the currently\n216: // running agent's output grow from inside the TUI. terminal is\n217: // config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached exactly like\n218: // the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's doc for the\n219: // override semantics. It returns the same short human description of where it\n220: // opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n221: //\n222: // Unlike Agent/Quick/Tig there is no mg-binary hop at all: `tail -f` is a\n223: // plain host command, so there is no ExeOverride resolution and no profile\n224: // flag. The inner command is deliberately NOT wrapped in holdOnFailure (the\n225: // one launch path that deviates): a user ends a tail pane with Ctrl+C (exit\n226: // 130, non-zero), so holding on a non-zero exit would leave a \"press enter to\n227: // close\" prompt every single time — the pane should just close when the user\n228: // is done watching.\n229: //\n230: // tail -f idles harmlessly once the underlying run ends (the file simply\n231: // stops growing), so there is no liveness check here; the caller decides when\n232: // the key and footer hint are reachable (e.g. gated on the log file existing).\n233: func Tail(logPath, terminal string) (string, error) {\n234: \tinner := tailShellCommand(logPath)\n235: \treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n236: }\n237: \n238: // ExeOverride returns the path of the binary implementing the mg session\n239: // subcommand — the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable\n240: // so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point Agent/Quick/AgentQuick\n241: // at a stub or a resolution failure instead of the real os.Executable().\n242: var ExeOverride = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n243: \n244: // TerminalSetting returns the user's configured terminal override — the\n245: // `terminal` field of config/tui-settings.json — or \"\" when unset or\n246: // unreadable. It is the default every terminal-launch path falls back to when\n247: // the caller passes an empty terminal (see launchDetached), so the setting is\n248: // honored from the TUI — which passes it explicitly — and from CLI paths like\n249: // `mg done`'s git-solver handoff alike, instead of silently degrading to\n250: // auto-detect. An exported package-level variable, mirroring ExeOverride, so\n251: // tests can stub it.\n252: var TerminalSetting = func() string {\n253: \ts, err := config.Load()\n254: \tif err != nil {\n255: \t\treturn \"\"\n256: \t}\n257: \treturn s.Terminal\n258: }\n259: \n260: // JdiExe returns the path of the binary implementing the `mg jdi` subcommand —\n261: // the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable, mirroring\n262: // ExeOverride, so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point it at a\n263: // stub or a resolution failure.\n264: var JdiExe = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n265: \n266: // TigLookPath resolves the tig binary on the host, mirroring `mg diff --tig`'s\n267: // own check (cmd/mg/diff.go's tigLookPath). An exported package-level\n268: // variable, mirroring ExeOverride/JdiExe, so tests (in this package and\n269: // internal/ui) can point it at a stub or a tig-less PATH instead of requiring\n270: // tig on the test machine.\n271: var TigLookPath = exec.LookPath\n272: \n273: // TigAvailable reports whether tig resolves on the host — the \"if available\"\n274: // gate the TUI's `t` key and footer hint key off (see Tig's own doc for why\n275: // the launch path re-checks this itself). It is deliberately a lightweight\n276: // LookPath probe with no version check or config scan: `mg diff --tig` is the\n277: // authoritative backstop and errors clearly on its own when tig is missing.\n278: func TigAvailable() bool {\n279: \t_, err := TigLookPath(\"tig\")\n280: \treturn err == nil\n281: }\n282: \n283: // Jdi starts `mg jdi --job <jobID> --profile <profile>` detached in the\n284: // background — no spawned terminal window at all, unlike Agent/Quick.\n285: // mg-jdi drives a fixed,\n286: // non-interactive agent sequence with no TTY at any point in its own\n287: // container invocations (the session launcher's --print flag) and needs no\n288: // terminal for a human or a subprocess to attach to — spawning one anyway\n289: // would be pure overhead and would reintroduce exactly the per-agent-window\n290: // cost the backlog's \"in-TUI agent terminal\" idea is about removing, not\n291: // adding to. Visibility into a TUI-launched run is the list-row status\n292: // badge and the detail-view log tab, not a window; see those for how a\n293: // human watches it run.\n294: //\n295: // profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles, matching\n296: // Agent/Quick — an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, mg-jdi's\n297: // own default (see cmd/mg/jdi.go).\n298: //\n299: // cmd.Dir stays on projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job lives\n300: // in its own worktree: mg-jdi — like every other\n301: // `--job` invocation — re-derives the effective worktree root itself from the\n302: // job id it is given, per invocation, so it must be launched from the project\n303: // root that find_project_root expects, never from a job worktree directly.\n304: // See Agent's doc for the full reasoning, shared by all four launch paths.\n305: //\n306: // Unlike Agent/Quick there is no \"where it opened\" description to return —\n307: // there is no terminal/pane to describe — so this returns only an error (nil\n308: // on a successful start). Like launchDetached, the started process's stdio is\n309: // discarded and it is reaped asynchronously so it cannot corrupt the TUI's\n310: // alt screen or zombie; mg-jdi may run for a long time (several full agent\n311: // sessions), so this must not block waiting for it.\n312: func Jdi(jobID, projectRoot, profile string) error {\n313: \tif profile == \"\" {\n314: \t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n315: \t}\n316: \texe, err := JdiExe()\n317: \tif err != nil {\n318: \t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n319: \t}\n320: \tcmd := exec.Command(exe, \"jdi\", \"--job\", jobID, \"--profile\", profile)\n321: \tcmd.Dir = projectRoot\n322: \tcmd.Stdin = nil\n323: \tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n324: \tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n325: \tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n326: \t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"start mg jdi: %w\", err)\n327: \t}\n328: \tif cmd.Process != nil {\n329: \t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n330: \t}\n331: \treturn nil\n332: }\n333: \n334: // tmuxPaneTag is the pane title manigot tags the split panes it opens with\n335: // (`tmux select-pane -t <id> -T`, run by launchTmuxPane after the split —\n336: // split-window itself has no -T on any released tmux). It is how a later\n337: // launch finds the pane manigot opened last so it can be replaced — see\n338: // killPreviousTmuxPane. It lives in the pane title (in tmux, not in memory)\n339: // so it survives a TUI restart.\n340: const tmuxPaneTag = \"manigot\"\n341: \n342: // tmuxMu serializes the find-kill-split sequence in launchTmuxPane so two\n343: // concurrent launches (Agent/Quick can be invoked from Bubble Tea command\n344: // goroutines) can never interleave: without it, both could list the same\n345: // previous pane, both split, and the \"at most one manigot pane\" invariant\n346: // would break. Also guards tmuxLastPaneID.\n347: var tmuxMu sync.Mutex\n348: \n349: // tmuxLastPaneID is the pane_id (e.g. \"%12\") of the most recent pane manigot\n350: // opened via launchTmuxPane, or \"\" if none is known yet (e.g. the TUI was\n351: // restarted after the pane was opened, or no launch has happened). Guarded by\n352: // tmuxMu.\n353: var tmuxLastPaneID string\n354: \n355: // launchTmuxPane opens the manigot session as a split pane in the TUI's\n356: // current tmux window and implements the \"replace\" reuse policy:\n357: // before splitting, the pane manigot opened last is killed so at most one\n358: // manigot pane exists at a time. It is the tmux-specific launch path — unlike\n359: // the generic launchDetached tail it runs the split-window client\n360: // synchronously because it needs its stdout (the new pane's id, via\n361: // -P -F '#{pane_id}') to tag the pane with tmuxPaneTag (select-pane -T, since\n362: // split-window has no -T on released tmux) and to record it for the next\n363: // replace; split-window itself returns as soon as the pane exists, the inner\n364: // command runs inside the pane, not in this client, so blocking on it is\n365: // milliseconds and Output() captures stdout+stderr so nothing leaks onto the\n366: // TUI's screen.\n367: func launchTmuxPane(inner string) (string, error) {\n368: \ttmuxMu.Lock()\n369: \tdefer tmuxMu.Unlock()\n370: \n371: \t// Replace, then split. The replace is best-effort: a failure to find the\n372: \t// previous pane must not prevent the new launch — if tmux is in a state\n373: \t// where list-panes fails, the split-window below will fail too and its\n374: \t// error is the accurate one to surface. The user having already closed\n375: \t// the previous pane is not an error at all (tolerated inside).\n376: \t_ = killPreviousTmuxPane()\n377: \n378: \tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, \"\")\n379: \tif err != nil {\n380: \t\treturn \"\", err\n381: \t}\n382: \tout, err := cmd.Output()\n383: \tif err != nil {\n384: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n385: \t}\n386: \tpaneID := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n387: \tif paneID == \"\" {\n388: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: tmux split-window printed no pane id\", desc)\n389: \t}\n390: \n391: \t// Tag the new pane so a later launch can find it even after a TUI restart\n392: \t// (which loses tmuxLastPaneID). select-pane -T is supported by every\n393: \t// released tmux, unlike split-window -T. Best-effort: the pane is already\n394: \t// live and the in-memory id covers replacement within this TUI process, so\n395: \t// a tagging failure only narrows the restart-surviving lookup — it must\n396: \t// not abort a launch that already succeeded.\n397: \t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"select-pane\", \"-t\", paneID, \"-T\", tmuxPaneTag).Run()\n398: \n399: \ttmuxLastPaneID = paneID\n400: \treturn desc, nil\n401: }\n402: \n403: // killPreviousTmuxPane implements the \"replace\" reuse policy: it kills\n404: // the split pane manigot opened last so that at most one manigot pane exists\n405: // at a time. The pane is identified two ways, both safe against ever killing\n406: // a pane manigot did not itself open:\n407: //\n408: //   - tmuxLastPaneID — the pane_id learned from the previous split-window's\n409: //     -P output; reliable while this TUI process lives.\n410: //   - any pane in the current session whose title is tmuxPaneTag — set via\n411: //     select-pane -T after the split (see launchTmuxPane); survives TUI\n412: //     restarts because it lives in tmux, not in memory (a restart loses\n413: //     tmuxLastPaneID).\n414: //\n415: // list-panes is scoped to the current session (-s): the manigot pane is split\n416: // from the TUI's own pane, so it lives in the TUI's session (a tmux client\n417: // run as a subprocess of the TUI resolves \"current session\" from $TMUX_PANE),\n418: // and -s will also find it if the user moved it to another window of that\n419: // session — without reaching into a different session where a different\n420: // manigot TUI might legitimately have its own pane. A pane the user already\n421: // closed in the meantime is not an error: that kill-pane failure is ignored.\n422: // The kill is unconditional with respect to the pane's state — a pane mid-\n423: // hold-on-failure is replaced exactly like a live session.\n424: // Called with tmuxMu held.\n425: func killPreviousTmuxPane() error {\n426: \tcandidates := map[string]struct{}{}\n427: \tif tmuxLastPaneID != \"\" {\n428: \t\tcandidates[tmuxLastPaneID] = struct{}{}\n429: \t}\n430: \tout, err := exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"list-panes\", \"-s\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id} #{pane_title}\").Output()\n431: \tif err != nil {\n432: \t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"tmux list-panes: %w\", err)\n433: \t}\n434: \tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), \"\\n\") {\n435: \t\tfields := strings.SplitN(line, \" \", 2)\n436: \t\tif len(fields) == 2 && fields[1] == tmuxPaneTag {\n437: \t\t\tcandidates[fields[0]] = struct{}{}\n438: \t\t}\n439: \t}\n440: \tfor id := range candidates {\n441: \t\t// Already closed by the user (or its window closed with it) → the\n442: \t\t// replace is a no-op, which is the tolerated case.\n443: \t\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"kill-pane\", \"-t\", id).Run()\n444: \t}\n445: \treturn nil\n446: }\n447: \n448: // launchDetached is the shared spawn/reap tail used by both launch paths: it\n449: // builds the *exec.Cmd for inner, discards its stdio so the launcher cannot\n450: // corrupt the TUI's alt screen, starts it, and reaps the launcher\n451: // asynchronously so it doesn't zombie (the actual terminal/pane outlives it).\n452: // It returns the short \"where it opened\" description buildCmd produces.\n453: //\n454: // terminal is config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty it is used in place of\n455: // the macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (branch 3/4 below) — but only when the TUI\n456: // is NOT inside tmux. Inside tmux (with a tmux binary on PATH) the launch is\n457: // handled by launchTmuxPane instead, regardless of the override: a user who\n458: // runs the TUI inside tmux always gets the split-pane behavior, and the\n459: // override applies only outside tmux.\n460: //\n461: // An empty terminal falls back to TerminalSetting — the user's configured\n462: // override itself — before auto-detect, so every launch path honors the same\n463: // setting even when the caller doesn't have it loaded (the TUI passes it\n464: // explicitly; CLI paths like `mg done`'s git-solver handoff pass \"\" and get\n465: // the setting here). Only when the setting is also unset does the auto-detect\n466: // chain below apply. Inside tmux the terminal value is never consulted, so\n467: // the resolution is harmless there — the tmux split pane wins regardless.\n468: //\n469: // Inside tmux, the launch is handled by launchTmuxPane: it implements the\n470: // replace policy (killing the previously manigot-opened pane before splitting\n471: // a new one) and needs the split-window client's stdout (the new pane's id),\n472: // so it runs synchronously rather than through the detached tail below. The\n473: // same tmux detection ($TMUX set and a tmux binary on PATH) is used here and\n474: // in buildCmd.\n475: //\n476: // See Agent's doc comment for why a launcher that starts successfully but then\n477: // fails on its own (e.g. no display server) is not surfaced here — holdOnFailure\n478: // covers the inner command's own fast failures instead.\n479: func launchDetached(inner, terminal string) (string, error) {\n480: \t// The caller's explicit terminal wins; an empty one falls back to the\n481: \t// user's configured override (TerminalSetting) so the setting is honored\n482: \t// from every launch path, TUI and CLI alike. Auto-detect applies only when\n483: \t// neither is set. Inside tmux the resolved value is never consulted — the\n484: \t// tmux split pane below wins regardless.\n485: \tif terminal == \"\" {\n486: \t\tterminal = TerminalSetting()\n487: \t}\n488: \n489: \t// Inside tmux with a tmux binary on PATH, the tmux split pane always wins —\n490: \t// even when an override is set. The override applies only outside tmux.\n491: \tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n492: \t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n493: \t\t\treturn launchTmuxPane(inner)\n494: \t\t}\n495: \t}\n496: \n497: \tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, terminal)\n498: \tif err != nil {\n499: \t\treturn \"\", err\n500: \t}\n501: \tcmd.Stdin = nil\n502: \tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n503: \tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n504: \tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n505: \t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n506: \t}\n507: \t// Reap the launcher to avoid a zombie; the actual terminal/pane outlives it.\n508: \tif cmd.Process != nil {\n509: \t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n510: \t}\n511: \treturn desc, nil\n512: }\n513: \n514: // shellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n515: //\n516: //\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --job '<jobID>'; ec=$?; ...\n517: //\n518: // cd-ing first matters because manigot finds the project root from $PWD (the\n519: // same walk-up job.FindProjectRoot uses). manigotPath is the absolute path from the\n520: // resolve package; it is quoted like every other value, so a checkout in a\n521: // directory with spaces survives both osascript and `bash -lc`. Arguments are\n522: // single-quoted and embedded single quotes are escaped, so no value can break\n523: // out of its quotes. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro so the\n524: // flag is always passed explicitly, regardless of what the session\n525: // launcher's own default happens to be.\n526: //\n527: // None of buildCmd's five spawn paths keep the\n528: // window/pane open once the inner command exits, success or failure — tmux\n529: // destroys a new window as soon as its command exits (no remain-on-exit),\n530: // Terminal.app's `do script` types the command but the window otherwise\n531: // behaves normally, and gnome-terminal/x-terminal-emulator/konsole/xterm all\n532: // close on exit by default. A fast failure (docker not running, resolve\n533: // failure inside the container, an auth error) is therefore invisible: the\n534: // window flashes and disappears before its output can be read — this is the\n535: // brief's \"a window appears and it immediately closes again\". The result is\n536: // wrapped in holdOnFailure so a non-zero exit pauses instead.\n537: func shellCommand(manigotPath, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n538: \tif profile == \"\" {\n539: \t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n540: \t}\n541: \tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s --job %s\",\n542: \t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(jobID))\n543: \treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n544: }\n545: \n546: // quickShellCommand builds the shell string for a bare manigot session (no\n547: // --agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n548: //\n549: //\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>'; ec=$?; ...\n550: //\n551: // It is the --agent/--job-less counterpart to shellCommand: same cd-first,\n552: // shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior (so a fast failure of the\n553: // inner command still holds the window open), just without the\n554: // agent/job flags. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the\n555: // same reason shellCommand does. It is deliberately a separate function rather\n556: // than a generalization of shellCommand so the latter's exact-format tests stay\n557: // unchanged.\n558: func quickShellCommand(manigotPath, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n559: \tif profile == \"\" {\n560: \t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n561: \t}\n562: \tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s\",\n563: \t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile))\n564: \treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n565: }\n566: \n567: // agentQuickShellCommand builds the shell string for AgentQuick's jobless\n568: // agent session (--agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n569: //\n570: //\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>'; ec=$?; ...\n571: //\n572: // It sits between shellCommand (agent + job) and quickShellCommand (neither):\n573: // same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior, just\n574: // with --agent and no --job. An empty profile defaults to\n575: // config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the other two do. Deliberately\n576: // its own function rather than a generalization of either existing one, per\n577: // the file's \"deliberately a separate function\" convention — keeps\n578: // shellCommand/quickShellCommand's exact-format tests stable.\n579: func agentQuickShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n580: \tif profile == \"\" {\n581: \t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n582: \t}\n583: \tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s\",\n584: \t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent))\n585: \treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n586: }\n587: \n588: // hostAgentShellCommand builds the shell string for HostAgent's host-mode\n589: // agent session (--agent, --prompt, no --job), executed inside the new\n590: // terminal:\n591: //\n592: //\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' host --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --prompt '<prompt>'; ec=$?; ...\n593: //\n594: // Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n595: // other launch paths, with the `host` subcommand inserted and the prompt\n596: // passed via --prompt — the one launch path that carries a prompt. The prompt\n597: // is a free-form multi-sentence instruction, so shellQuote's embedded-quote\n598: // escaping matters here more than anywhere (see TestHostAgentShellCommandQuotesPrompt).\n599: // An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the\n600: // other builders do. Deliberately its own function rather than a\n601: // generalization of the other shell-command builders, per the file's\n602: // \"deliberately separate function per launch path\" convention.\n603: func hostAgentShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n604: \tif profile == \"\" {\n605: \t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n606: \t}\n607: \tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s host --profile %s --agent %s --prompt %s\",\n608: \t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(prompt))\n609: \treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n610: }\n611: \n612: // tigShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n613: //\n614: //\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' diff '<jobID>' --tig; ec=$?; ...\n615: //\n616: // Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n617: // other launch paths — the job's files are resolved by `mg diff` from $PWD,\n618: // so cd-ing first matters exactly as it does for Agent/Quick. No --profile\n619: // flag: `mg diff` takes none (it is a host git command, not a session\n620: // launch). Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the\n621: // other shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch\n622: // path\" convention — keeps their exact-format tests unchanged.\n623: func tigShellCommand(manigotPath, jobID, projectRoot string) string {\n624: \tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s diff %s --tig\",\n625: \t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(jobID))\n626: \treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n627: }\n628: \n629: // tailShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n630: //\n631: //\ttail -f '<logPath>'\n632: //\n633: // The log path is absolute (the caller passes job.JDIRunLogPath's output), so\n634: // there is no cd-first like the other launch paths — and the inner command is\n635: // deliberately NOT wrapped in holdOnFailure, the one deviation from the\n636: // agent/quick/tig convention: a user ends `tail -f` with Ctrl+C (exit 130,\n637: // non-zero), so holding on a non-zero exit would leave a \"press enter to\n638: // close\" prompt every single time instead of just closing the pane. Same\n639: // shellQuote-everything behavior as the other builders — a path with spaces\n640: // or embedded quotes survives osascript and `bash -lc` as one word.\n641: // Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the other\n642: // shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch path\"\n643: // convention.\n644: func tailShellCommand(logPath string) string {\n645: \treturn \"tail -f \" + shellQuote(logPath)\n646: }\n647: \n648: // holdOnFailure wraps a shell command so that, if it exits non-zero, the\n649: // window/pane it is running in stays open with the failure message visible\n650: // instead of closing immediately. A clean exit (status 0) is left\n651: // alone — the launched agent session usually runs interactively until the\n652: // user ends it themselves, so closing right away once that happens is the\n653: // expected behavior; only a fast, invisible failure needs holding open.\n654: //\n655: // Wrapping the shared inner command string (rather than reaching for a\n656: // per-terminal mechanism like tmux's remain-on-exit or `xterm -hold`) keeps\n657: // the fix uniform across all five spawn paths in buildCmd and lets it be\n658: // exercised by ordinary string-based tests.\n659: //\n660: // The wrap behaves identically inside a tmux split pane as in a\n661: // window — tmux runs the pane's shell-command in a pty and destroys the pane\n662: // when the command exits, so the `read -r _ignored` blocking on a non-zero\n663: // exit keeps the pane open with the failure message visible exactly as it\n664: // held a new window open (the message may wrap in the narrower 35% pane, but\n665: // stays fully visible). And because the replace policy treats every\n666: // manigot pane alike, a pane mid-hold-on-failure is killed by a subsequent\n667: // launch just like a live session is — deliberate and unconditional (the\n668: // \"skip or warn?\" alternative was rejected: skipping would break\n669: // the at-most-one-pane invariant and needs unfeasible mid-hold detection from\n670: // outside the pane, and warning needs new TUI plumbing that is out of scope\n671: // for this job). A user who launches something new has moved on from the\n672: // failure they were present for when they launched it.\n673: func holdOnFailure(cmd string) string {\n674: \treturn cmd + `; ec=$?; if [ \"$ec\" -ne 0 ]; then echo; echo \"--- manigot: exited with status $ec ---\"; printf 'press enter to close... '; read -r _ignored; fi; exit \"$ec\"`\n675: }\n676: \n677: // shellQuote wraps s in single quotes, escaping any embedded single quote via\n678: // the standard '\\” idiom.\n679: func shellQuote(s string) string {\n680: \treturn \"'\" + strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"'\", `'\\''`) + \"'\"\n681: }\n682: \n683: // terminalCandidates is the Linux terminal-emulator candidate table used both\n684: // by buildCmd's auto-detect chain (branch 4) and, by name lookup, by\n685: // buildOverrideCmd's known-name-vs-\"-e\"-fallback flag-convention choice:\n686: // an override whose binary matches one of these names\n687: // (case-insensitively) reuses that name's own flag convention instead of the\n688: // \"-e\" default.\n689: var terminalCandidates = []struct {\n690: \tname string\n691: \tpre  []string // args between the emulator name and the shell command\n692: }{\n693: \t{\"gnome-terminal\", []string{\"--\"}},\n694: \t{\"ptyxis\", []string{\"--\"}},\n695: \t{\"x-terminal-emulator\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n696: \t{\"konsole\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n697: \t{\"xterm\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n698: }\n699: \n700: // buildOverrideCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for an explicit user-chosen\n701: // terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal): it takes over the auto-detect\n702: // spawn order — the tmux branch is only reachable inside tmux, where\n703: // launchDetached/buildCmd prefer the tmux pane, so an override is invoked when\n704: // the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there), replacing the\n705: // macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (see launchDetached).\n706: //\n707: // terminal's first whitespace-separated token is the binary to run; any\n708: // remaining tokens are passed as leading arguments before the flag that hands\n709: // off the inner shell command — mirroring how config.Settings.Editor (and\n710: // $VISUAL/$EDITOR) already allow trailing args, e.g. \"code --wait\". The\n711: // binary is looked up via exec.LookPath exactly like the auto-detect\n712: // candidates in terminalCandidates, so a typo'd or missing binary surfaces a\n713: // clear \"launch <name>: exec: ... file not found\" error here instead of\n714: // silently falling back to auto-detect.\n715: //\n716: // The flag used to hand off the inner command is chosen the same way as the\n717: // existing candidates: if the binary case-insensitively matches one of\n718: // terminalCandidates' own names, that name's convention (\"--\" or \"-e\") is\n719: // reused; otherwise \"-e\" is used, since it is the more common convention\n720: // among terminals not already in that list (kitty, alacritty, wezterm, rxvt,\n721: // terminator all accept it). This is a best-effort convention, not a\n722: // guarantee for every possible terminal emulator — a known limitation, not\n723: // solved generally, since a fully general solution would need a per-terminal\n724: // flag field the brief did not ask for.\n725: func buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n726: \tfields := strings.Fields(terminal)\n727: \tif len(fields) == 0 {\n728: \t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"terminal override is blank\")\n729: \t}\n730: \tname := fields[0]\n731: \tleadingArgs := fields[1:]\n732: \n733: \tif _, err := exec.LookPath(name); err != nil {\n734: \t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", name, err)\n735: \t}\n736: \n737: \tflag := \"-e\"\n738: \tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n739: \t\tif strings.EqualFold(c.name, name) {\n740: \t\t\tflag = c.pre[0]\n741: \t\t\tbreak\n742: \t\t}\n743: \t}\n744: \n745: \targs := append(append([]string{}, leadingArgs...), flag, \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner)\n746: \treturn exec.Command(name, args...), name, nil\n747: }\n748: \n749: // buildCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for the detected environment, or — when\n750: // terminal (config.Settings.Terminal) is non-empty and the TUI is not inside\n751: // tmux — for the explicit override instead (see buildOverrideCmd). The tmux\n752: // branch (1) is checked first and always wins when the TUI is inside tmux and\n753: // a tmux binary is on PATH, even over an override; branches 2–4 (override,\n754: // macOS Terminal.app, Linux emulators) run detached via launchDetached's\n755: // spawn/reap tail. The tmux branch is also the command launchTmuxPane runs\n756: // synchronously.\n757: func buildCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n758: \t// 1. Inside tmux (works on every OS that has tmux): split a pane off the\n759: \t// TUI's current window, replacing the previously manigot-opened\n760: \t// pane. Checked before the override so a session launched from a TUI\n761: \t// inside tmux always lands in a tmux pane, regardless of\n762: \t// config.Settings.Terminal.\n763: \tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n764: \t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n765: \t\t\t// -h splits side by side (vertical divider); -l 35% sizes the new\n766: \t\t\t// pane to 35% of the window width so the TUI's own pane (run\n767: \t\t\t// with no explicit -t, so this targets the TUI's own currently\n768: \t\t\t// active pane) keeps the majority share.\n769: \t\t\t// -l (not the older -p) is required: tmux deprecated -p's\n770: \t\t\t// percentage sizing in 3.1 in favor of -l with a \"%\" suffix, and\n771: \t\t\t// tmux 3.4 rejects -p outright with \"size missing\" (surfaced to\n772: \t\t\t// the user as the split-window client exiting 1) instead of\n773: \t\t\t// splitting.\n774: \t\t\t// -P -F '#{pane_id}' makes the split-window client print the new\n775: \t\t\t// pane's id to its stdout so launchTmuxPane can tag it with\n776: \t\t\t// tmuxPaneTag (via select-pane -T — split-window has no -T on any\n777: \t\t\t// released tmux) and record it for the replace policy.\n778: \t\t\t//\n779: \t\t\t// The trailing \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner (three separate argv elements,\n780: \t\t\t// not one combined string) matters: given a single shell-command\n781: \t\t\t// argument, tmux runs it via the pane's default-shell in -c mode,\n782: \t\t\t// which is non-login and non-interactive — .zshrc/.bash_profile are\n783: \t\t\t// never sourced, so any PATH customization living there (docker,\n784: \t\t\t// a manigot install dir, nvm, homebrew, ...) is invisible to it and\n785: \t\t\t// the inner command fails fast (caught by holdOnFailure, but the\n786: \t\t\t// session never actually starts). Passing multiple argv elements\n787: \t\t\t// instead makes tmux exec them directly with no shell in between,\n788: \t\t\t// so this becomes an ordinary `bash -lc inner` invocation — the\n789: \t\t\t// exact login-shell wrapping every other spawn path below already\n790: \t\t\t// uses (see shellArgs), just constructed inline since tmux's\n791: \t\t\t// argv-vs-single-string parsing is what selects shell-vs-direct-exec\n792: \t\t\t// here, unlike a plain exec.Command call.\n793: \t\t\treturn exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"split-window\", \"-h\", \"-l\", \"35%\",\n794: \t\t\t\t\"-P\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id}\", \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner), \"tmux pane\", nil\n795: \t\t}\n796: \t}\n797: \n798: \t// 2. The explicit terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal), when the\n799: \t// TUI is not inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there).\n800: \tif terminal != \"\" {\n801: \t\treturn buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal)\n802: \t}\n803: \n804: \t// 3. macOS Terminal.app.\n805: \tif runtime.GOOS == \"darwin\" {\n806: \t\tscript := fmt.Sprintf(`tell application \"Terminal\" to do script %q`, inner)\n807: \t\treturn exec.Command(\"osascript\", \"-e\", script), \"Terminal.app\", nil\n808: \t}\n809: \n810: \t// 4. Linux terminal emulators, in order of preference.\n811: \tshellArgs := []string{\"bash\", \"-lc\", inner}\n812: \tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n813: \t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(c.name); err == nil {\n814: \t\t\targs := append(append([]string{}, c.pre...), shellArgs...)\n815: \t\t\treturn exec.Command(c.name, args...), c.name, nil\n816: \t\t}\n817: \t}\n818: \n819: \treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"no supported terminal launcher found (looked for: tmux, Terminal.app, gnome-terminal, ptyxis, x-terminal-emulator, konsole, xterm)\")\n820: }\n\n(End of file - total 820 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"// Package launch spawns manigot agent sessions in a new terminal window or\n// pane so the TUI can keep running while the agent works.\n//\n// Spawn order (macOS + Linux; no Windows v1):\n//  1. Inside tmux — when the TUI is itself running inside tmux (detected via\n//     the $TMUX env var, with a tmux binary on PATH), this works the same on\n//     every platform. The session is opened as a split pane in the TUI's\n//     current window (`tmux split-window), replacing the pane manigot opened\n//     last so at most one manigot pane exists at a time (the replace policy).\n//     Inside tmux this branch always wins — even when\n//     config.Settings.Terminal is set — so a session launched from a TUI\n//     that is itself inside tmux lands in a tmux pane, never in a separate\n//     window.\n//  2. config.Settings.Terminal, when set: the explicit user-chosen terminal\n//     (e.g. \"kitty\") is invoked directly — see buildOverrideCmd. It applies\n//     only when the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is not available there);\n//     inside tmux the split pane above wins.\n//  3. macOS Terminal.app via osascript.\n//  4. A Linux terminal emulator, tried in order: gnome-terminal, ptyxis\n//     (Fedora's default GNOME terminal since Fedora 41), x-terminal-emulator","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/src/internal/launch/launch.go","text":"// Package launch spawns manigot agent sessions in a new terminal window or\n// pane so the TUI can keep running while the agent works.\n//\n// Spawn order (macOS + Linux; no Windows v1):\n//  1. Inside tmux — when the TUI is itself running inside tmux (detected via\n//     the $TMUX env var, with a tmux binary on PATH), this works the same on\n//     every platform. The session is opened as a split pane in the TUI's\n//     current window (`tmux split-window), replacing the pane manigot opened\n//     last so at most one manigot pane exists at a time (the replace policy).\n//     Inside tmux this branch always wins — even when\n//     config.Settings.Terminal is set — so a session launched from a TUI\n//     that is itself inside tmux lands in a tmux pane, never in a separate\n//     window.\n//  2. config.Settings.Terminal, when set: the explicit user-chosen terminal\n//     (e.g. \"kitty\") is invoked directly — see buildOverrideCmd. It applies\n//     only when the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is not available there);\n//     inside tmux the split pane above wins.\n//  3. macOS Terminal.app via osascript.\n//  4. A Linux terminal emulator, tried in order: gnome-terminal, ptyxis\n//     (Fedora's default GNOME terminal since Fedora 41), x-terminal-emulator\n//     (Debian), konsole, xterm.\n//\n// The command opened is always `<mg-binary> --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` run\n// from the project root, matching the session launcher's invocation contract.\n// <mg-binary> is the running binary's own path (os.Executable) — the one Go\n// binary IS the command now — so an install under a different name still\n// works.\npackage launch\n\nimport (\n\t\"errors\"\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"runtime\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"sync\"\n\n\t\"github.com/lmuskalla/manigot/internal/config\"\n)\n\n// Agent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window (see launchTmuxPane) — that runs manigot with `--profile <profile>\n// --agent <agent> --job <jobID>` in projectRoot. profile is one of the\n// subscription profiles in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to\n// config.ProfileClaudePro, matching the session launcher's own default. terminal is\n// config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty and the TUI is NOT inside tmux, that\n// terminal is invoked directly instead of the auto-detect chain below — see\n// buildOverrideCmd; inside tmux the split pane wins regardless of this setting.\n// Empty falls back to the configured override itself (TerminalSetting), so\n// callers that don't have the setting loaded — CLI paths like `mg done`'s\n// git-solver handoff — still honor it; only when the setting is also unset does\n// the auto-detect chain apply. It returns a short\n// human description of where it opened (e.g. \"tmux pane\", \"Terminal.app\",\n// \"gnome-terminal\", or the override's own binary name) so the caller can surface\n// it in a status line.\n//\n// The launcher runs from projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job\n// lives in its own worktree: the session launcher\n// (session.ResolveRootFrom) re-derives the effective mount root itself from\n// the --job it is given (branch-match + worktree lookup, hard error on a\n// worktree-less job), so launching from projectRoot is not just sufficient\n// but required — it locates the project from $PWD and only then resolves the\n// job's worktree. The same reasoning keeps Quick/AgentQuick (no --job at all, mount\n// PROJECT_ROOT directly) and Jdi (mg-jdi, which re-derives per invocation)\n// on projectRoot.\n//\n// The launcher process is detached: its stdio is discarded so it cannot\n// corrupt the TUI's alt screen, and it is reaped asynchronously. The one\n// exception is the tmux path, where the split-window client is run\n// synchronously (see launchTmuxPane) so its pane-id output can be captured\n// for the replace policy — split-window itself returns in milliseconds, it\n// does not wait for the agent session.\n//\n// cmd.Start() failing (the launcher binary itself couldn't be\n// spawned — e.g. permission denied) is already surfaced: it's returned as an\n// error here and shown by the caller (App.updateDetail via cmdErrorText). The\n// remaining gap is a launcher that starts successfully but then fails on its\n// own after Agent has already returned \"success\" to the caller — e.g.\n// gnome-terminal/konsole exiting non-zero because there's no display server.\n// Surfacing that would need either blocking on cmd.Wait() here or wiring the\n// reaping goroutine back into a tea.Msg, and neither is safe to add: xterm\n// (unlike gnome-terminal/konsole/tmux/osascript, which detach quickly) *is*\n// the window process, so it doesn't return until the window closes — for it,\n// Wait()-ing here would block Update() for the whole agent session, which is\n// strictly worse than the bug being fixed. holdOnFailure\n// already covers the failure mode the brief actually reports (the inner `sc\n// --agent` command failing fast); a launcher-binary-itself failure is a\n// narrower, rarer case left uncovered rather than risk reintroducing UI lag.\nfunc Agent(agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := shellCommand(exe, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// Quick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window, exactly like Agent, including the replace policy — that runs manigot\n// with just `--profile <profile>` (no --agent, no --job) in projectRoot: a\n// bare session for an ad-hoc change that doesn't belong to any specific\n// job/agent workflow. profile is one of the subscription profiles in\n// config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n// matching Agent. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to\n// launchDetached exactly like Agent's own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n// doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n// of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// The session launcher treats --agent and --job as optional, so a bare `sc\n// --profile <profile>` simply runs claude/opencode against the current project\n// with no agent flag and no job prompt.\nfunc Quick(projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := quickShellCommand(exe, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// AgentQuick opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n// current window, exactly like Agent/Quick, including the replace policy —\n// that runs manigot with `--profile <profile> --agent <agent>` (no --job) in\n// projectRoot: launching a specific agent for an ad-hoc session that doesn't\n// belong to any particular job. profile is one of the subscription profiles\n// in config.Profiles; an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro,\n// matching Agent/Quick. terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded\n// through to launchDetached exactly like Agent/Quick's own terminal\n// parameter — see Agent's doc for the override semantics. It returns the\n// same short human description of where it opened so the caller can surface\n// it in a status line.\n//\n// The session launcher treats --job as optional (see Quick's own doc for the\n// --agent/--job-less case), so `sc --profile <profile> --agent <agent>`\n// simply runs that agent against the current project with no job prompt.\nfunc AgentQuick(agent, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := agentQuickShellCommand(exe, agent, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// HostAgent opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's\n// current window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace\n// policy — that runs `mg host --profile <profile> --agent <agent> --prompt\n// '<prompt>'` in projectRoot: a host-mode agent session (the agent CLI runs\n// directly on the host, unisolated — see `mg host`) with an explicit prompt.\n// prompt is the free-form instruction the agent starts with (the session\n// launcher passes it through verbatim); it is what `mg done` uses to hand a\n// failed squash merge to @git-solver, telling it what broke and what cleanup\n// remains. profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles;\n// an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, matching Agent/Quick.\n// terminal is config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached\n// exactly like the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's\n// doc for the override semantics. It returns the same short human description\n// of where it opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/AgentQuick there is no --job: host-mode sessions run against\n// the project root itself — the main worktree, where a failed `mg done`\n// squash merge leaves its conflicted state — never a job worktree.\nfunc HostAgent(agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := hostAgentShellCommand(exe, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// Tig opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window, exactly like Agent/Quick/AgentQuick, including the replace policy —\n// that runs `mg diff '<jobName>' --tig` in projectRoot: the host-side tig TUI\n// browsing what the job's branch changed (the CLI path already exists —\n// cmd/mg/diff.go runTig resolves the job's branch + the project's base\n// branch, builds the three-dot range <base>...<branch>, runs `tig <range>`,\n// and errors clearly when tig is not installed). terminal is\n// config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached exactly like\n// the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's doc for the\n// override semantics. It returns the same short human description of where it\n// opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no profile flag: `mg diff` is a plain host git\n// command, not a session launch, so it takes no --profile (and no --agent or\n// --job either).\n//\n// The job is passed by its Name (id_slug), not its ID: `mg diff` resolves the\n// job's branch via an exact match on the branch's id_slug tail segment first,\n// then a unique prefix (cmd/mg/diff.go resolveJobBranch) — and job.Name\n// equals that tail segment exactly, so resolution is unambiguous.\n//\n// Tig re-checks availability itself (TigAvailable) before spawning: the\n// TUI's cached availability gate (detailView.tigAvailable) is only as fresh\n// as the last job open, so a stale cached \"available\" must surface a\n// synchronous error here rather than opening a doomed pane that dies on the\n// CLI's own tig-missing check.\nfunc Tig(jobID, projectRoot, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\tif !TigAvailable() {\n\t\treturn \"\", errors.New(\"tig is not installed on the host — install it, or use the diff tab\")\n\t}\n\texe, err := ExeOverride()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tinner := tigShellCommand(exe, jobID, projectRoot)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// Tail opens a new terminal — inside tmux, a split pane in the TUI's current\n// window, exactly like Agent/Quick/Tig, including the replace policy — that\n// runs `tail -f '<logPath>'`: a live tail of the given absolute log file (the\n// mg-jdi run.log sidecar, job.JDIRunLogPath) so a user can watch the currently\n// running agent's output grow from inside the TUI. terminal is\n// config.Settings.Terminal, threaded through to launchDetached exactly like\n// the other launch paths' own terminal parameter — see Agent's doc for the\n// override semantics. It returns the same short human description of where it\n// opened so the caller can surface it in a status line.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/Quick/Tig there is no mg-binary hop at all: `tail -f` is a\n// plain host command, so there is no ExeOverride resolution and no profile\n// flag. The inner command is deliberately NOT wrapped in holdOnFailure (the\n// one launch path that deviates): a user ends a tail pane with Ctrl+C (exit\n// 130, non-zero), so holding on a non-zero exit would leave a \"press enter to\n// close\" prompt every single time — the pane should just close when the user\n// is done watching.\n//\n// tail -f idles harmlessly once the underlying run ends (the file simply\n// stops growing), so there is no liveness check here; the caller decides when\n// the key and footer hint are reachable (e.g. gated on the log file existing).\nfunc Tail(logPath, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\tinner := tailShellCommand(logPath)\n\treturn launchDetached(inner, terminal)\n}\n\n// ExeOverride returns the path of the binary implementing the mg session\n// subcommand — the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable\n// so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point Agent/Quick/AgentQuick\n// at a stub or a resolution failure instead of the real os.Executable().\nvar ExeOverride = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n\n// TerminalSetting returns the user's configured terminal override — the\n// `terminal` field of config/tui-settings.json — or \"\" when unset or\n// unreadable. It is the default every terminal-launch path falls back to when\n// the caller passes an empty terminal (see launchDetached), so the setting is\n// honored from the TUI — which passes it explicitly — and from CLI paths like\n// `mg done`'s git-solver handoff alike, instead of silently degrading to\n// auto-detect. An exported package-level variable, mirroring ExeOverride, so\n// tests can stub it.\nvar TerminalSetting = func() string {\n\ts, err := config.Load()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\"\n\t}\n\treturn s.Terminal\n}\n\n// JdiExe returns the path of the binary implementing the `mg jdi` subcommand —\n// the running binary itself. An exported package-level variable, mirroring\n// ExeOverride, so tests (in this package and internal/ui) can point it at a\n// stub or a resolution failure.\nvar JdiExe = func() (string, error) { return os.Executable() }\n\n// TigLookPath resolves the tig binary on the host, mirroring `mg diff --tig`'s\n// own check (cmd/mg/diff.go's tigLookPath). An exported package-level\n// variable, mirroring ExeOverride/JdiExe, so tests (in this package and\n// internal/ui) can point it at a stub or a tig-less PATH instead of requiring\n// tig on the test machine.\nvar TigLookPath = exec.LookPath\n\n// TigAvailable reports whether tig resolves on the host — the \"if available\"\n// gate the TUI's `t` key and footer hint key off (see Tig's own doc for why\n// the launch path re-checks this itself). It is deliberately a lightweight\n// LookPath probe with no version check or config scan: `mg diff --tig` is the\n// authoritative backstop and errors clearly on its own when tig is missing.\nfunc TigAvailable() bool {\n\t_, err := TigLookPath(\"tig\")\n\treturn err == nil\n}\n\n// Jdi starts `mg jdi --job <jobID> --profile <profile>` detached in the\n// background — no spawned terminal window at all, unlike Agent/Quick.\n// mg-jdi drives a fixed,\n// non-interactive agent sequence with no TTY at any point in its own\n// container invocations (the session launcher's --print flag) and needs no\n// terminal for a human or a subprocess to attach to — spawning one anyway\n// would be pure overhead and would reintroduce exactly the per-agent-window\n// cost the backlog's \"in-TUI agent terminal\" idea is about removing, not\n// adding to. Visibility into a TUI-launched run is the list-row status\n// badge and the detail-view log tab, not a window; see those for how a\n// human watches it run.\n//\n// profile is one of the subscription profiles in config.Profiles, matching\n// Agent/Quick — an empty value defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro, mg-jdi's\n// own default (see cmd/mg/jdi.go).\n//\n// cmd.Dir stays on projectRoot (the main worktree) even though the job lives\n// in its own worktree: mg-jdi — like every other\n// `--job` invocation — re-derives the effective worktree root itself from the\n// job id it is given, per invocation, so it must be launched from the project\n// root that find_project_root expects, never from a job worktree directly.\n// See Agent's doc for the full reasoning, shared by all four launch paths.\n//\n// Unlike Agent/Quick there is no \"where it opened\" description to return —\n// there is no terminal/pane to describe — so this returns only an error (nil\n// on a successful start). Like launchDetached, the started process's stdio is\n// discarded and it is reaped asynchronously so it cannot corrupt the TUI's\n// alt screen or zombie; mg-jdi may run for a long time (several full agent\n// sessions), so this must not block waiting for it.\nfunc Jdi(jobID, projectRoot, profile string) error {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\texe, err := JdiExe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"locate mg binary: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tcmd := exec.Command(exe, \"jdi\", \"--job\", jobID, \"--profile\", profile)\n\tcmd.Dir = projectRoot\n\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"start mg jdi: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// tmuxPaneTag is the pane title manigot tags the split panes it opens with\n// (`tmux select-pane -t <id> -T`, run by launchTmuxPane after the split —\n// split-window itself has no -T on any released tmux). It is how a later\n// launch finds the pane manigot opened last so it can be replaced — see\n// killPreviousTmuxPane. It lives in the pane title (in tmux, not in memory)\n// so it survives a TUI restart.\nconst tmuxPaneTag = \"manigot\"\n\n// tmuxMu serializes the find-kill-split sequence in launchTmuxPane so two\n// concurrent launches (Agent/Quick can be invoked from Bubble Tea command\n// goroutines) can never interleave: without it, both could list the same\n// previous pane, both split, and the \"at most one manigot pane\" invariant\n// would break. Also guards tmuxLastPaneID.\nvar tmuxMu sync.Mutex\n\n// tmuxLastPaneID is the pane_id (e.g. \"%12\") of the most recent pane manigot\n// opened via launchTmuxPane, or \"\" if none is known yet (e.g. the TUI was\n// restarted after the pane was opened, or no launch has happened). Guarded by\n// tmuxMu.\nvar tmuxLastPaneID string\n\n// launchTmuxPane opens the manigot session as a split pane in the TUI's\n// current tmux window and implements the \"replace\" reuse policy:\n// before splitting, the pane manigot opened last is killed so at most one\n// manigot pane exists at a time. It is the tmux-specific launch path — unlike\n// the generic launchDetached tail it runs the split-window client\n// synchronously because it needs its stdout (the new pane's id, via\n// -P -F '#{pane_id}') to tag the pane with tmuxPaneTag (select-pane -T, since\n// split-window has no -T on released tmux) and to record it for the next\n// replace; split-window itself returns as soon as the pane exists, the inner\n// command runs inside the pane, not in this client, so blocking on it is\n// milliseconds and Output() captures stdout+stderr so nothing leaks onto the\n// TUI's screen.\nfunc launchTmuxPane(inner string) (string, error) {\n\ttmuxMu.Lock()\n\tdefer tmuxMu.Unlock()\n\n\t// Replace, then split. The replace is best-effort: a failure to find the\n\t// previous pane must not prevent the new launch — if tmux is in a state\n\t// where list-panes fails, the split-window below will fail too and its\n\t// error is the accurate one to surface. The user having already closed\n\t// the previous pane is not an error at all (tolerated inside).\n\t_ = killPreviousTmuxPane()\n\n\tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, \"\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tout, err := cmd.Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n\t}\n\tpaneID := strings.TrimSpace(string(out))\n\tif paneID == \"\" {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: tmux split-window printed no pane id\", desc)\n\t}\n\n\t// Tag the new pane so a later launch can find it even after a TUI restart\n\t// (which loses tmuxLastPaneID). select-pane -T is supported by every\n\t// released tmux, unlike split-window -T. Best-effort: the pane is already\n\t// live and the in-memory id covers replacement within this TUI process, so\n\t// a tagging failure only narrows the restart-surviving lookup — it must\n\t// not abort a launch that already succeeded.\n\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"select-pane\", \"-t\", paneID, \"-T\", tmuxPaneTag).Run()\n\n\ttmuxLastPaneID = paneID\n\treturn desc, nil\n}\n\n// killPreviousTmuxPane implements the \"replace\" reuse policy: it kills\n// the split pane manigot opened last so that at most one manigot pane exists\n// at a time. The pane is identified two ways, both safe against ever killing\n// a pane manigot did not itself open:\n//\n//   - tmuxLastPaneID — the pane_id learned from the previous split-window's\n//     -P output; reliable while this TUI process lives.\n//   - any pane in the current session whose title is tmuxPaneTag — set via\n//     select-pane -T after the split (see launchTmuxPane); survives TUI\n//     restarts because it lives in tmux, not in memory (a restart loses\n//     tmuxLastPaneID).\n//\n// list-panes is scoped to the current session (-s): the manigot pane is split\n// from the TUI's own pane, so it lives in the TUI's session (a tmux client\n// run as a subprocess of the TUI resolves \"current session\" from $TMUX_PANE),\n// and -s will also find it if the user moved it to another window of that\n// session — without reaching into a different session where a different\n// manigot TUI might legitimately have its own pane. A pane the user already\n// closed in the meantime is not an error: that kill-pane failure is ignored.\n// The kill is unconditional with respect to the pane's state — a pane mid-\n// hold-on-failure is replaced exactly like a live session.\n// Called with tmuxMu held.\nfunc killPreviousTmuxPane() error {\n\tcandidates := map[string]struct{}{}\n\tif tmuxLastPaneID != \"\" {\n\t\tcandidates[tmuxLastPaneID] = struct{}{}\n\t}\n\tout, err := exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"list-panes\", \"-s\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id} #{pane_title}\").Output()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"tmux list-panes: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\tfor _, line := range strings.Split(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), \"\\n\") {\n\t\tfields := strings.SplitN(line, \" \", 2)\n\t\tif len(fields) == 2 && fields[1] == tmuxPaneTag {\n\t\t\tcandidates[fields[0]] = struct{}{}\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfor id := range candidates {\n\t\t// Already closed by the user (or its window closed with it) → the\n\t\t// replace is a no-op, which is the tolerated case.\n\t\t_ = exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"kill-pane\", \"-t\", id).Run()\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// launchDetached is the shared spawn/reap tail used by both launch paths: it\n// builds the *exec.Cmd for inner, discards its stdio so the launcher cannot\n// corrupt the TUI's alt screen, starts it, and reaps the launcher\n// asynchronously so it doesn't zombie (the actual terminal/pane outlives it).\n// It returns the short \"where it opened\" description buildCmd produces.\n//\n// terminal is config.Settings.Terminal: when non-empty it is used in place of\n// the macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (branch 3/4 below) — but only when the TUI\n// is NOT inside tmux. Inside tmux (with a tmux binary on PATH) the launch is\n// handled by launchTmuxPane instead, regardless of the override: a user who\n// runs the TUI inside tmux always gets the split-pane behavior, and the\n// override applies only outside tmux.\n//\n// An empty terminal falls back to TerminalSetting — the user's configured\n// override itself — before auto-detect, so every launch path honors the same\n// setting even when the caller doesn't have it loaded (the TUI passes it\n// explicitly; CLI paths like `mg done`'s git-solver handoff pass \"\" and get\n// the setting here). Only when the setting is also unset does the auto-detect\n// chain below apply. Inside tmux the terminal value is never consulted, so\n// the resolution is harmless there — the tmux split pane wins regardless.\n//\n// Inside tmux, the launch is handled by launchTmuxPane: it implements the\n// replace policy (killing the previously manigot-opened pane before splitting\n// a new one) and needs the split-window client's stdout (the new pane's id),\n// so it runs synchronously rather than through the detached tail below. The\n// same tmux detection ($TMUX set and a tmux binary on PATH) is used here and\n// in buildCmd.\n//\n// See Agent's doc comment for why a launcher that starts successfully but then\n// fails on its own (e.g. no display server) is not surfaced here — holdOnFailure\n// covers the inner command's own fast failures instead.\nfunc launchDetached(inner, terminal string) (string, error) {\n\t// The caller's explicit terminal wins; an empty one falls back to the\n\t// user's configured override (TerminalSetting) so the setting is honored\n\t// from every launch path, TUI and CLI alike. Auto-detect applies only when\n\t// neither is set. Inside tmux the resolved value is never consulted — the\n\t// tmux split pane below wins regardless.\n\tif terminal == \"\" {\n\t\tterminal = TerminalSetting()\n\t}\n\n\t// Inside tmux with a tmux binary on PATH, the tmux split pane always wins —\n\t// even when an override is set. The override applies only outside tmux.\n\tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n\t\t\treturn launchTmuxPane(inner)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tcmd, desc, err := buildCmd(inner, terminal)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", err\n\t}\n\tcmd.Stdin = nil\n\tcmd.Stdout = io.Discard\n\tcmd.Stderr = io.Discard\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", desc, err)\n\t}\n\t// Reap the launcher to avoid a zombie; the actual terminal/pane outlives it.\n\tif cmd.Process != nil {\n\t\tgo func() { _ = cmd.Wait() }()\n\t}\n\treturn desc, nil\n}\n\n// shellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --job '<jobID>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// cd-ing first matters because manigot finds the project root from $PWD (the\n// same walk-up job.FindProjectRoot uses). manigotPath is the absolute path from the\n// resolve package; it is quoted like every other value, so a checkout in a\n// directory with spaces survives both osascript and `bash -lc`. Arguments are\n// single-quoted and embedded single quotes are escaped, so no value can break\n// out of its quotes. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro so the\n// flag is always passed explicitly, regardless of what the session\n// launcher's own default happens to be.\n//\n// None of buildCmd's five spawn paths keep the\n// window/pane open once the inner command exits, success or failure — tmux\n// destroys a new window as soon as its command exits (no remain-on-exit),\n// Terminal.app's `do script` types the command but the window otherwise\n// behaves normally, and gnome-terminal/x-terminal-emulator/konsole/xterm all\n// close on exit by default. A fast failure (docker not running, resolve\n// failure inside the container, an auth error) is therefore invisible: the\n// window flashes and disappears before its output can be read — this is the\n// brief's \"a window appears and it immediately closes again\". The result is\n// wrapped in holdOnFailure so a non-zero exit pauses instead.\nfunc shellCommand(manigotPath, agent, jobID, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s --job %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(jobID))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// quickShellCommand builds the shell string for a bare manigot session (no\n// --agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// It is the --agent/--job-less counterpart to shellCommand: same cd-first,\n// shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior (so a fast failure of the\n// inner command still holds the window open), just without the\n// agent/job flags. An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the\n// same reason shellCommand does. It is deliberately a separate function rather\n// than a generalization of shellCommand so the latter's exact-format tests stay\n// unchanged.\nfunc quickShellCommand(manigotPath, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// agentQuickShellCommand builds the shell string for AgentQuick's jobless\n// agent session (--agent, no --job), executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// It sits between shellCommand (agent + job) and quickShellCommand (neither):\n// same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior, just\n// with --agent and no --job. An empty profile defaults to\n// config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the other two do. Deliberately\n// its own function rather than a generalization of either existing one, per\n// the file's \"deliberately a separate function\" convention — keeps\n// shellCommand/quickShellCommand's exact-format tests stable.\nfunc agentQuickShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s --profile %s --agent %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// hostAgentShellCommand builds the shell string for HostAgent's host-mode\n// agent session (--agent, --prompt, no --job), executed inside the new\n// terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' host --profile '<profile>' --agent '<agent>' --prompt '<prompt>'; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n// other launch paths, with the `host` subcommand inserted and the prompt\n// passed via --prompt — the one launch path that carries a prompt. The prompt\n// is a free-form multi-sentence instruction, so shellQuote's embedded-quote\n// escaping matters here more than anywhere (see TestHostAgentShellCommandQuotesPrompt).\n// An empty profile defaults to config.ProfileClaudePro for the same reason the\n// other builders do. Deliberately its own function rather than a\n// generalization of the other shell-command builders, per the file's\n// \"deliberately separate function per launch path\" convention.\nfunc hostAgentShellCommand(manigotPath, agent, prompt, projectRoot, profile string) string {\n\tif profile == \"\" {\n\t\tprofile = config.ProfileClaudePro\n\t}\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s host --profile %s --agent %s --prompt %s\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(profile), shellQuote(agent), shellQuote(prompt))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// tigShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\tcd '<projectRoot>' && '<manigot>' diff '<jobID>' --tig; ec=$?; ...\n//\n// Same cd-first, shellQuote-everything, holdOnFailure-wrap behavior as the\n// other launch paths — the job's files are resolved by `mg diff` from $PWD,\n// so cd-ing first matters exactly as it does for Agent/Quick. No --profile\n// flag: `mg diff` takes none (it is a host git command, not a session\n// launch). Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the\n// other shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch\n// path\" convention — keeps their exact-format tests unchanged.\nfunc tigShellCommand(manigotPath, jobID, projectRoot string) string {\n\tinner := fmt.Sprintf(\"cd %s && %s diff %s --tig\",\n\t\tshellQuote(projectRoot), shellQuote(manigotPath), shellQuote(jobID))\n\treturn holdOnFailure(inner)\n}\n\n// tailShellCommand builds the shell string executed inside the new terminal:\n//\n//\ttail -f '<logPath>'\n//\n// The log path is absolute (the caller passes job.JDIRunLogPath's output), so\n// there is no cd-first like the other launch paths — and the inner command is\n// deliberately NOT wrapped in holdOnFailure, the one deviation from the\n// agent/quick/tig convention: a user ends `tail -f` with Ctrl+C (exit 130,\n// non-zero), so holding on a non-zero exit would leave a \"press enter to\n// close\" prompt every single time instead of just closing the pane. Same\n// shellQuote-everything behavior as the other builders — a path with spaces\n// or embedded quotes survives osascript and `bash -lc` as one word.\n// Deliberately its own function rather than a generalization of the other\n// shell-command builders, per the file's \"separate function per launch path\"\n// convention.\nfunc tailShellCommand(logPath string) string {\n\treturn \"tail -f \" + shellQuote(logPath)\n}\n\n// holdOnFailure wraps a shell command so that, if it exits non-zero, the\n// window/pane it is running in stays open with the failure message visible\n// instead of closing immediately. A clean exit (status 0) is left\n// alone — the launched agent session usually runs interactively until the\n// user ends it themselves, so closing right away once that happens is the\n// expected behavior; only a fast, invisible failure needs holding open.\n//\n// Wrapping the shared inner command string (rather than reaching for a\n// per-terminal mechanism like tmux's remain-on-exit or `xterm -hold`) keeps\n// the fix uniform across all five spawn paths in buildCmd and lets it be\n// exercised by ordinary string-based tests.\n//\n// The wrap behaves identically inside a tmux split pane as in a\n// window — tmux runs the pane's shell-command in a pty and destroys the pane\n// when the command exits, so the `read -r _ignored` blocking on a non-zero\n// exit keeps the pane open with the failure message visible exactly as it\n// held a new window open (the message may wrap in the narrower 35% pane, but\n// stays fully visible). And because the replace policy treats every\n// manigot pane alike, a pane mid-hold-on-failure is killed by a subsequent\n// launch just like a live session is — deliberate and unconditional (the\n// \"skip or warn?\" alternative was rejected: skipping would break\n// the at-most-one-pane invariant and needs unfeasible mid-hold detection from\n// outside the pane, and warning needs new TUI plumbing that is out of scope\n// for this job). A user who launches something new has moved on from the\n// failure they were present for when they launched it.\nfunc holdOnFailure(cmd string) string {\n\treturn cmd + `; ec=$?; if [ \"$ec\" -ne 0 ]; then echo; echo \"--- manigot: exited with status $ec ---\"; printf 'press enter to close... '; read -r _ignored; fi; exit \"$ec\"`\n}\n\n// shellQuote wraps s in single quotes, escaping any embedded single quote via\n// the standard '\\” idiom.\nfunc shellQuote(s string) string {\n\treturn \"'\" + strings.ReplaceAll(s, \"'\", `'\\''`) + \"'\"\n}\n\n// terminalCandidates is the Linux terminal-emulator candidate table used both\n// by buildCmd's auto-detect chain (branch 4) and, by name lookup, by\n// buildOverrideCmd's known-name-vs-\"-e\"-fallback flag-convention choice:\n// an override whose binary matches one of these names\n// (case-insensitively) reuses that name's own flag convention instead of the\n// \"-e\" default.\nvar terminalCandidates = []struct {\n\tname string\n\tpre  []string // args between the emulator name and the shell command\n}{\n\t{\"gnome-terminal\", []string{\"--\"}},\n\t{\"ptyxis\", []string{\"--\"}},\n\t{\"x-terminal-emulator\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n\t{\"konsole\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n\t{\"xterm\", []string{\"-e\"}},\n}\n\n// buildOverrideCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for an explicit user-chosen\n// terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal): it takes over the auto-detect\n// spawn order — the tmux branch is only reachable inside tmux, where\n// launchDetached/buildCmd prefer the tmux pane, so an override is invoked when\n// the TUI is NOT inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there), replacing the\n// macOS/Linux auto-detect chain (see launchDetached).\n//\n// terminal's first whitespace-separated token is the binary to run; any\n// remaining tokens are passed as leading arguments before the flag that hands\n// off the inner shell command — mirroring how config.Settings.Editor (and\n// $VISUAL/$EDITOR) already allow trailing args, e.g. \"code --wait\". The\n// binary is looked up via exec.LookPath exactly like the auto-detect\n// candidates in terminalCandidates, so a typo'd or missing binary surfaces a\n// clear \"launch <name>: exec: ... file not found\" error here instead of\n// silently falling back to auto-detect.\n//\n// The flag used to hand off the inner command is chosen the same way as the\n// existing candidates: if the binary case-insensitively matches one of\n// terminalCandidates' own names, that name's convention (\"--\" or \"-e\") is\n// reused; otherwise \"-e\" is used, since it is the more common convention\n// among terminals not already in that list (kitty, alacritty, wezterm, rxvt,\n// terminator all accept it). This is a best-effort convention, not a\n// guarantee for every possible terminal emulator — a known limitation, not\n// solved generally, since a fully general solution would need a per-terminal\n// flag field the brief did not ask for.\nfunc buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n\tfields := strings.Fields(terminal)\n\tif len(fields) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"terminal override is blank\")\n\t}\n\tname := fields[0]\n\tleadingArgs := fields[1:]\n\n\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(name); err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"launch %s: %w\", name, err)\n\t}\n\n\tflag := \"-e\"\n\tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n\t\tif strings.EqualFold(c.name, name) {\n\t\t\tflag = c.pre[0]\n\t\t\tbreak\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\targs := append(append([]string{}, leadingArgs...), flag, \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner)\n\treturn exec.Command(name, args...), name, nil\n}\n\n// buildCmd constructs the *exec.Cmd for the detected environment, or — when\n// terminal (config.Settings.Terminal) is non-empty and the TUI is not inside\n// tmux — for the explicit override instead (see buildOverrideCmd). The tmux\n// branch (1) is checked first and always wins when the TUI is inside tmux and\n// a tmux binary is on PATH, even over an override; branches 2–4 (override,\n// macOS Terminal.app, Linux emulators) run detached via launchDetached's\n// spawn/reap tail. The tmux branch is also the command launchTmuxPane runs\n// synchronously.\nfunc buildCmd(inner, terminal string) (*exec.Cmd, string, error) {\n\t// 1. Inside tmux (works on every OS that has tmux): split a pane off the\n\t// TUI's current window, replacing the previously manigot-opened\n\t// pane. Checked before the override so a session launched from a TUI\n\t// inside tmux always lands in a tmux pane, regardless of\n\t// config.Settings.Terminal.\n\tif os.Getenv(\"TMUX\") != \"\" {\n\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(\"tmux\"); err == nil {\n\t\t\t// -h splits side by side (vertical divider); -l 35% sizes the new\n\t\t\t// pane to 35% of the window width so the TUI's own pane (run\n\t\t\t// with no explicit -t, so this targets the TUI's own currently\n\t\t\t// active pane) keeps the majority share.\n\t\t\t// -l (not the older -p) is required: tmux deprecated -p's\n\t\t\t// percentage sizing in 3.1 in favor of -l with a \"%\" suffix, and\n\t\t\t// tmux 3.4 rejects -p outright with \"size missing\" (surfaced to\n\t\t\t// the user as the split-window client exiting 1) instead of\n\t\t\t// splitting.\n\t\t\t// -P -F '#{pane_id}' makes the split-window client print the new\n\t\t\t// pane's id to its stdout so launchTmuxPane can tag it with\n\t\t\t// tmuxPaneTag (via select-pane -T — split-window has no -T on any\n\t\t\t// released tmux) and record it for the replace policy.\n\t\t\t//\n\t\t\t// The trailing \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner (three separate argv elements,\n\t\t\t// not one combined string) matters: given a single shell-command\n\t\t\t// argument, tmux runs it via the pane's default-shell in -c mode,\n\t\t\t// which is non-login and non-interactive — .zshrc/.bash_profile are\n\t\t\t// never sourced, so any PATH customization living there (docker,\n\t\t\t// a manigot install dir, nvm, homebrew, ...) is invisible to it and\n\t\t\t// the inner command fails fast (caught by holdOnFailure, but the\n\t\t\t// session never actually starts). Passing multiple argv elements\n\t\t\t// instead makes tmux exec them directly with no shell in between,\n\t\t\t// so this becomes an ordinary `bash -lc inner` invocation — the\n\t\t\t// exact login-shell wrapping every other spawn path below already\n\t\t\t// uses (see shellArgs), just constructed inline since tmux's\n\t\t\t// argv-vs-single-string parsing is what selects shell-vs-direct-exec\n\t\t\t// here, unlike a plain exec.Command call.\n\t\t\treturn exec.Command(\"tmux\", \"split-window\", \"-h\", \"-l\", \"35%\",\n\t\t\t\t\"-P\", \"-F\", \"#{pane_id}\", \"bash\", \"-lc\", inner), \"tmux pane\", nil\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\t// 2. The explicit terminal override (config.Settings.Terminal), when the\n\t// TUI is not inside tmux (or tmux is unavailable there).\n\tif terminal != \"\" {\n\t\treturn buildOverrideCmd(inner, terminal)\n\t}\n\n\t// 3. macOS Terminal.app.\n\tif runtime.GOOS == \"darwin\" {\n\t\tscript := fmt.Sprintf(`tell application \"Terminal\" to do script %q`, inner)\n\t\treturn exec.Command(\"osascript\", \"-e\", script), \"Terminal.app\", nil\n\t}\n\n\t// 4. Linux terminal emulators, in order of preference.\n\tshellArgs := []string{\"bash\", \"-lc\", inner}\n\tfor _, c := range terminalCandidates {\n\t\tif _, err := exec.LookPath(c.name); err == nil {\n\t\t\targs := append(append([]string{}, c.pre...), shellArgs...)\n\t\t\treturn exec.Command(c.name, args...), c.name, nil\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil, \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"no supported terminal launcher found (looked for: tmux, Terminal.app, gnome-terminal, ptyxis, x-terminal-emulator, konsole, xterm)\")\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":820,"totalLines":820,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/internal/launch/launch.go","time":{"start":1787919598184,"end":1787919598194}},"id":"prt_0485001ee001zILV2hP4KDozNr","sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","messageID":"msg_0484ffc03001UObwaP6Ap88UZ0"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787919598374,"sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01_ET_HXTmKASwqEeMVRzlzQvp3112","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/src/internal/job/jdistatus.go"},"output":"<path>/workspace/src/internal/job/jdistatus.go</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: package job\n2: \n3: import (\n4: \t\"encoding/json\"\n5: \t\"io\"\n6: \t\"os\"\n7: \t\"path/filepath\"\n8: \t\"strings\"\n9: \t\"time\"\n10: )\n11: \n12: // ManigotRelDir is manigot's own directory inside a target project, relative\n13: // to the project root: the home for host-side tooling state that is not job\n14: // content and does not belong in docs/. .manigot/manigot.json holds the\n15: // committable project settings (see the project package); .manigot/jdi-status/\n16: // holds mg-jdi's ephemeral run state (gitignored, see .gitignore).\n17: const ManigotRelDir = \".manigot\"\n18: \n19: // JDISidecarDirName is the sidecar directory mg-jdi writes its per-job\n20: // status and run log into, under ManigotRelDir — deliberately outside any job's own directory\n21: // (docs/jobs/<name>/) so it can never be swept into an agent's own\n22: // `git add -A`, and gitignored entirely (see .gitignore).\n23: const JDISidecarDirName = \"jdi-status\"\n24: \n25: // jdiStatusFileName / jdiRunLogFileName are the two files living under each\n26: // job's JDIStatusDir.\n27: const (\n28: \tjdiStatusFileName = \"status\"\n29: \tjdiRunLogFileName = \"run.log\"\n30: )\n31: \n32: // JDIState is mg-jdi's reported run state for a job, read from its status\n33: // sidecar file.\n34: type JDIState string\n35: \n36: const (\n37: \t// JDIRunning means mg-jdi is (or, absent a fresher update, very\n38: \t// recently was) actively driving this job.\n39: \tJDIRunning JDIState = \"running\"\n40: \t// JDIStoppedFinished means mg-jdi's last run ended because the job's\n41: \t// verdict was APPROVED.\n42: \tJDIStoppedFinished JDIState = \"stopped:finished\"\n43: \t// JDIStoppedNeedsHuman means mg-jdi's last run ended because it needed\n44: \t// a human (retry budget exhausted, a NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT marker, or the\n45: \t// stall backstop — see mg-jdi).\n46: \tJDIStoppedNeedsHuman JDIState = \"stopped:needs-human\"\n47: )\n48: \n49: // jdiRunningStaleAfter bounds how long a JDIRunning status is trusted\n50: // without an update before ReadJDIStatus degrades it to \"no run in\n51: // progress\". mg-jdi only updates the status file at\n52: // agent-invocation boundaries, not continuously while one is in flight, so\n53: // this must be generous enough that a single long-running agent call isn't\n54: // mistaken for a killed mg-jdi process.\n55: const jdiRunningStaleAfter = 30 * time.Minute\n56: \n57: // JDIStatus is mg-jdi's last-known status for a job.\n58: type JDIStatus struct {\n59: \tState   JDIState\n60: \tAgent   string\n61: \tUpdated time.Time\n62: }\n63: \n64: // jdiStatusFile is the on-disk JSON shape of the status sidecar file.\n65: type jdiStatusFile struct {\n66: \tState   JDIState `json:\"state\"`\n67: \tAgent   string   `json:\"agent\"`\n68: \tUpdated string   `json:\"updated\"` // RFC3339\n69: }\n70: \n71: // JDIStatusDir returns the sidecar directory for job jobName under root.\n72: func JDIStatusDir(root, jobName string) string {\n73: \treturn filepath.Join(root, ManigotRelDir, JDISidecarDirName, jobName)\n74: }\n75: \n76: // JDIStatusPath returns the status file path within JDIStatusDir.\n77: func JDIStatusPath(root, jobName string) string {\n78: \treturn filepath.Join(JDIStatusDir(root, jobName), jdiStatusFileName)\n79: }\n80: \n81: // JDIRunLogPath returns the run.log path within JDIStatusDir — the\n82: // append-only transcript mg-jdi writes, and the TUI's log tab reads.\n83: func JDIRunLogPath(root, jobName string) string {\n84: \treturn filepath.Join(JDIStatusDir(root, jobName), jdiRunLogFileName)\n85: }\n86: \n87: // ReadJDIStatus reads job jobName's mg-jdi status sidecar under root. ok is\n88: // false when there is nothing live to report: no sidecar file exists, it\n89: // can't be parsed, its state isn't one of the three known values, or it\n90: // claims JDIRunning but hasn't been updated in over jdiRunningStaleAfter\n91: // (mg-jdi was almost certainly killed mid-run). A stale or\n92: // unreadable file must never be shown as if it were live — this is the\n93: // normal path through which callers should read it, the one exception being\n94: // StaleRunningJDI's raw read for mg-jdi's own next-start crash check.\n95: func ReadJDIStatus(root, jobName string) (JDIStatus, bool) {\n96: \tst, ok := readJDIStatusRaw(root, jobName)\n97: \tif !ok {\n98: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n99: \t}\n100: \tif st.State == JDIRunning && time.Since(st.Updated) > jdiRunningStaleAfter {\n101: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n102: \t}\n103: \treturn st, true\n104: }\n105: \n106: // readJDIStatusRaw is the shared parse of the status sidecar: it returns the\n107: // parsed status with ok=false when the file is missing, unparseable, or\n108: // holds an unknown state value. Unlike ReadJDIStatus it does NOT degrade a\n109: // stale JDIRunning entry away — staleness is the caller's business.\n110: func readJDIStatusRaw(root, jobName string) (JDIStatus, bool) {\n111: \tdata, err := os.ReadFile(JDIStatusPath(root, jobName))\n112: \tif err != nil {\n113: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n114: \t}\n115: \n116: \tvar raw jdiStatusFile\n117: \tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &raw); err != nil {\n118: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n119: \t}\n120: \n121: \tupdated, err := time.Parse(time.RFC3339, raw.Updated)\n122: \tif err != nil {\n123: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n124: \t}\n125: \n126: \tswitch raw.State {\n127: \tcase JDIRunning, JDIStoppedFinished, JDIStoppedNeedsHuman:\n128: \tdefault:\n129: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n130: \t}\n131: \n132: \treturn JDIStatus{State: raw.State, Agent: raw.Agent, Updated: updated}, true\n133: }\n134: \n135: // StaleRunningJDI reports whether job jobName's mg-jdi sidecar claims a run\n136: // is in progress (JDIRunning) but has not been updated in over\n137: // jdiRunningStaleAfter — the on-disk signature of an mg-jdi process that was\n138: // killed (SIGKILL, OOM, host reboot) without ever reporting a stop. ok is\n139: // true only for that stale-running case: a stopped:* state, a fresh running\n140: // state, or a missing/unparseable sidecar all report ok=false. It is the raw\n141: // counterpart to ReadJDIStatus's degradation (same staleness constant, same\n142: // parse) and exists so mg-jdi's next-start crash check (see\n143: // cmd/mg/jdi.go's notifyCrashedRun) can distinguish \"no run in progress\"\n144: // from \"a run died mid-flight\".\n145: func StaleRunningJDI(root, jobName string) (JDIStatus, bool) {\n146: \tst, ok := readJDIStatusRaw(root, jobName)\n147: \tif !ok || st.State != JDIRunning {\n148: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n149: \t}\n150: \tif time.Since(st.Updated) <= jdiRunningStaleAfter {\n151: \t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n152: \t}\n153: \treturn st, true\n154: }\n155: \n156: // WriteJDIStatus writes/overwrites job jobName's status sidecar under root.\n157: // Called by mg-jdi at each state transition: once before\n158: // invoking an agent (JDIRunning, that agent's name) and once more at loop\n159: // exit (JDIStoppedFinished or JDIStoppedNeedsHuman, the last agent that\n160: // ran). Lives here so the write and read sides share\n161: // one on-disk format definition rather than risking drift between two\n162: // independent copies.\n163: func WriteJDIStatus(root, jobName string, state JDIState, agent string) error {\n164: \tdir := JDIStatusDir(root, jobName)\n165: \tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o755); err != nil {\n166: \t\treturn err\n167: \t}\n168: \traw := jdiStatusFile{\n169: \t\tState:   state,\n170: \t\tAgent:   agent,\n171: \t\tUpdated: time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339),\n172: \t}\n173: \tdata, err := json.Marshal(raw)\n174: \tif err != nil {\n175: \t\treturn err\n176: \t}\n177: \treturn os.WriteFile(JDIStatusPath(root, jobName), data, 0o644)\n178: }\n179: \n180: // RemoveJDIStatus removes job jobName's mg-jdi sidecar directory under root —\n181: // the status file and run.log that mg-jdi leaves behind. The job-destruction\n182: // lifecycle paths (DeleteJob, FinishJob, and the orphan removers) call it so\n183: // the tool cleans up after itself instead of leaving .manigot/jdi-status/<job>/\n184: // accumulating forever after the job itself is gone. removed reports whether a\n185: // sidecar actually existed — a job mg-jdi never drove has no sidecar, and its\n186: // absence is not an error.\n187: func RemoveJDIStatus(root, jobName string) (removed bool, err error) {\n188: \tdir := JDIStatusDir(root, jobName)\n189: \tif _, err := os.Stat(dir); err != nil {\n190: \t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n191: \t\t\treturn false, nil\n192: \t\t}\n193: \t\treturn false, err\n194: \t}\n195: \treturn true, os.RemoveAll(dir)\n196: }\n197: \n198: // jdiRunLogTailBytes bounds how much of a job's run.log ReadJDIRunLogTail\n199: // loads into memory — a tail, not \"load everything\" (a large/growing\n200: // file), the same kind of bounded-viewport\n201: // approach the markdown viewer already uses for job files. Generous enough\n202: // to comfortably hold many invocations' worth of text while staying a small,\n203: // fixed cost regardless of how long a job's full history grows.\n204: const jdiRunLogTailBytes = 256 * 1024 // 256 KiB\n205: \n206: // ReadJDIRunLogTail reads up to the last jdiRunLogTailBytes of job jobName's\n207: // run.log sidecar (JDIRunLogPath) — the TUI's log tab, the primary way to\n208: // see what mg-jdi is doing from inside the TUI.\n209: //\n210: // ok is false only when there is no run.log at all yet — the common case of\n211: // a job mg-jdi has never driven — so callers can show a distinct \"no run\n212: // yet\" placeholder instead of an empty tab. An existing-but-empty file (the\n213: // sidecar directory exists but no invocation has completed yet) returns\n214: // (\"\", true): still \"there is a run happening,\" just nothing logged yet.\n215: func ReadJDIRunLogTail(root, jobName string) (content string, ok bool) {\n216: \tf, err := os.Open(JDIRunLogPath(root, jobName))\n217: \tif err != nil {\n218: \t\treturn \"\", false\n219: \t}\n220: \tdefer f.Close()\n221: \n222: \tinfo, err := f.Stat()\n223: \tif err != nil {\n224: \t\treturn \"\", false\n225: \t}\n226: \tsize := info.Size()\n227: \tif size == 0 {\n228: \t\treturn \"\", true\n229: \t}\n230: \n231: \tstart := int64(0)\n232: \ttruncated := false\n233: \tif size > jdiRunLogTailBytes {\n234: \t\tstart = size - jdiRunLogTailBytes\n235: \t\ttruncated = true\n236: \t}\n237: \tif _, err := f.Seek(start, io.SeekStart); err != nil {\n238: \t\treturn \"\", false\n239: \t}\n240: \tdata, err := io.ReadAll(f)\n241: \tif err != nil {\n242: \t\treturn \"\", false\n243: \t}\n244: \n245: \ttext := string(data)\n246: \tif truncated {\n247: \t\t// Drop a possibly-partial first line so the tail starts cleanly at\n248: \t\t// an invocation boundary rather than mid-line.\n249: \t\tif idx := strings.IndexByte(text, '\\n'); idx >= 0 {\n250: \t\t\ttext = text[idx+1:]\n251: \t\t}\n252: \t\ttext = \"… (log truncated — showing the most recent portion)\\n\\n\" + text\n253: \t}\n254: \treturn text, true\n255: }\n\n(End of file - total 255 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"package job\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// ManigotRelDir is manigot's own directory inside a target project, relative\n// to the project root: the home for host-side tooling state that is not job\n// content and does not belong in docs/. .manigot/manigot.json holds the\n// committable project settings (see the project package); .manigot/jdi-status/\n// holds mg-jdi's ephemeral run state (gitignored, see .gitignore).\nconst ManigotRelDir = \".manigot\"\n\n// JDISidecarDirName is the sidecar directory mg-jdi writes its per-job\n// status and run log into, under ManigotRelDir — deliberately outside any job's own directory","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/src/internal/job/jdistatus.go","text":"package job\n\nimport (\n\t\"encoding/json\"\n\t\"io\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"time\"\n)\n\n// ManigotRelDir is manigot's own directory inside a target project, relative\n// to the project root: the home for host-side tooling state that is not job\n// content and does not belong in docs/. .manigot/manigot.json holds the\n// committable project settings (see the project package); .manigot/jdi-status/\n// holds mg-jdi's ephemeral run state (gitignored, see .gitignore).\nconst ManigotRelDir = \".manigot\"\n\n// JDISidecarDirName is the sidecar directory mg-jdi writes its per-job\n// status and run log into, under ManigotRelDir — deliberately outside any job's own directory\n// (docs/jobs/<name>/) so it can never be swept into an agent's own\n// `git add -A`, and gitignored entirely (see .gitignore).\nconst JDISidecarDirName = \"jdi-status\"\n\n// jdiStatusFileName / jdiRunLogFileName are the two files living under each\n// job's JDIStatusDir.\nconst (\n\tjdiStatusFileName = \"status\"\n\tjdiRunLogFileName = \"run.log\"\n)\n\n// JDIState is mg-jdi's reported run state for a job, read from its status\n// sidecar file.\ntype JDIState string\n\nconst (\n\t// JDIRunning means mg-jdi is (or, absent a fresher update, very\n\t// recently was) actively driving this job.\n\tJDIRunning JDIState = \"running\"\n\t// JDIStoppedFinished means mg-jdi's last run ended because the job's\n\t// verdict was APPROVED.\n\tJDIStoppedFinished JDIState = \"stopped:finished\"\n\t// JDIStoppedNeedsHuman means mg-jdi's last run ended because it needed\n\t// a human (retry budget exhausted, a NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT marker, or the\n\t// stall backstop — see mg-jdi).\n\tJDIStoppedNeedsHuman JDIState = \"stopped:needs-human\"\n)\n\n// jdiRunningStaleAfter bounds how long a JDIRunning status is trusted\n// without an update before ReadJDIStatus degrades it to \"no run in\n// progress\". mg-jdi only updates the status file at\n// agent-invocation boundaries, not continuously while one is in flight, so\n// this must be generous enough that a single long-running agent call isn't\n// mistaken for a killed mg-jdi process.\nconst jdiRunningStaleAfter = 30 * time.Minute\n\n// JDIStatus is mg-jdi's last-known status for a job.\ntype JDIStatus struct {\n\tState   JDIState\n\tAgent   string\n\tUpdated time.Time\n}\n\n// jdiStatusFile is the on-disk JSON shape of the status sidecar file.\ntype jdiStatusFile struct {\n\tState   JDIState `json:\"state\"`\n\tAgent   string   `json:\"agent\"`\n\tUpdated string   `json:\"updated\"` // RFC3339\n}\n\n// JDIStatusDir returns the sidecar directory for job jobName under root.\nfunc JDIStatusDir(root, jobName string) string {\n\treturn filepath.Join(root, ManigotRelDir, JDISidecarDirName, jobName)\n}\n\n// JDIStatusPath returns the status file path within JDIStatusDir.\nfunc JDIStatusPath(root, jobName string) string {\n\treturn filepath.Join(JDIStatusDir(root, jobName), jdiStatusFileName)\n}\n\n// JDIRunLogPath returns the run.log path within JDIStatusDir — the\n// append-only transcript mg-jdi writes, and the TUI's log tab reads.\nfunc JDIRunLogPath(root, jobName string) string {\n\treturn filepath.Join(JDIStatusDir(root, jobName), jdiRunLogFileName)\n}\n\n// ReadJDIStatus reads job jobName's mg-jdi status sidecar under root. ok is\n// false when there is nothing live to report: no sidecar file exists, it\n// can't be parsed, its state isn't one of the three known values, or it\n// claims JDIRunning but hasn't been updated in over jdiRunningStaleAfter\n// (mg-jdi was almost certainly killed mid-run). A stale or\n// unreadable file must never be shown as if it were live — this is the\n// normal path through which callers should read it, the one exception being\n// StaleRunningJDI's raw read for mg-jdi's own next-start crash check.\nfunc ReadJDIStatus(root, jobName string) (JDIStatus, bool) {\n\tst, ok := readJDIStatusRaw(root, jobName)\n\tif !ok {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\tif st.State == JDIRunning && time.Since(st.Updated) > jdiRunningStaleAfter {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\treturn st, true\n}\n\n// readJDIStatusRaw is the shared parse of the status sidecar: it returns the\n// parsed status with ok=false when the file is missing, unparseable, or\n// holds an unknown state value. Unlike ReadJDIStatus it does NOT degrade a\n// stale JDIRunning entry away — staleness is the caller's business.\nfunc readJDIStatusRaw(root, jobName string) (JDIStatus, bool) {\n\tdata, err := os.ReadFile(JDIStatusPath(root, jobName))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\n\tvar raw jdiStatusFile\n\tif err := json.Unmarshal(data, &raw); err != nil {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\n\tupdated, err := time.Parse(time.RFC3339, raw.Updated)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\n\tswitch raw.State {\n\tcase JDIRunning, JDIStoppedFinished, JDIStoppedNeedsHuman:\n\tdefault:\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\n\treturn JDIStatus{State: raw.State, Agent: raw.Agent, Updated: updated}, true\n}\n\n// StaleRunningJDI reports whether job jobName's mg-jdi sidecar claims a run\n// is in progress (JDIRunning) but has not been updated in over\n// jdiRunningStaleAfter — the on-disk signature of an mg-jdi process that was\n// killed (SIGKILL, OOM, host reboot) without ever reporting a stop. ok is\n// true only for that stale-running case: a stopped:* state, a fresh running\n// state, or a missing/unparseable sidecar all report ok=false. It is the raw\n// counterpart to ReadJDIStatus's degradation (same staleness constant, same\n// parse) and exists so mg-jdi's next-start crash check (see\n// cmd/mg/jdi.go's notifyCrashedRun) can distinguish \"no run in progress\"\n// from \"a run died mid-flight\".\nfunc StaleRunningJDI(root, jobName string) (JDIStatus, bool) {\n\tst, ok := readJDIStatusRaw(root, jobName)\n\tif !ok || st.State != JDIRunning {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\tif time.Since(st.Updated) <= jdiRunningStaleAfter {\n\t\treturn JDIStatus{}, false\n\t}\n\treturn st, true\n}\n\n// WriteJDIStatus writes/overwrites job jobName's status sidecar under root.\n// Called by mg-jdi at each state transition: once before\n// invoking an agent (JDIRunning, that agent's name) and once more at loop\n// exit (JDIStoppedFinished or JDIStoppedNeedsHuman, the last agent that\n// ran). Lives here so the write and read sides share\n// one on-disk format definition rather than risking drift between two\n// independent copies.\nfunc WriteJDIStatus(root, jobName string, state JDIState, agent string) error {\n\tdir := JDIStatusDir(root, jobName)\n\tif err := os.MkdirAll(dir, 0o755); err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\traw := jdiStatusFile{\n\t\tState:   state,\n\t\tAgent:   agent,\n\t\tUpdated: time.Now().UTC().Format(time.RFC3339),\n\t}\n\tdata, err := json.Marshal(raw)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn os.WriteFile(JDIStatusPath(root, jobName), data, 0o644)\n}\n\n// RemoveJDIStatus removes job jobName's mg-jdi sidecar directory under root —\n// the status file and run.log that mg-jdi leaves behind. The job-destruction\n// lifecycle paths (DeleteJob, FinishJob, and the orphan removers) call it so\n// the tool cleans up after itself instead of leaving .manigot/jdi-status/<job>/\n// accumulating forever after the job itself is gone. removed reports whether a\n// sidecar actually existed — a job mg-jdi never drove has no sidecar, and its\n// absence is not an error.\nfunc RemoveJDIStatus(root, jobName string) (removed bool, err error) {\n\tdir := JDIStatusDir(root, jobName)\n\tif _, err := os.Stat(dir); err != nil {\n\t\tif os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\treturn false, nil\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn false, err\n\t}\n\treturn true, os.RemoveAll(dir)\n}\n\n// jdiRunLogTailBytes bounds how much of a job's run.log ReadJDIRunLogTail\n// loads into memory — a tail, not \"load everything\" (a large/growing\n// file), the same kind of bounded-viewport\n// approach the markdown viewer already uses for job files. Generous enough\n// to comfortably hold many invocations' worth of text while staying a small,\n// fixed cost regardless of how long a job's full history grows.\nconst jdiRunLogTailBytes = 256 * 1024 // 256 KiB\n\n// ReadJDIRunLogTail reads up to the last jdiRunLogTailBytes of job jobName's\n// run.log sidecar (JDIRunLogPath) — the TUI's log tab, the primary way to\n// see what mg-jdi is doing from inside the TUI.\n//\n// ok is false only when there is no run.log at all yet — the common case of\n// a job mg-jdi has never driven — so callers can show a distinct \"no run\n// yet\" placeholder instead of an empty tab. An existing-but-empty file (the\n// sidecar directory exists but no invocation has completed yet) returns\n// (\"\", true): still \"there is a run happening,\" just nothing logged yet.\nfunc ReadJDIRunLogTail(root, jobName string) (content string, ok bool) {\n\tf, err := os.Open(JDIRunLogPath(root, jobName))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", false\n\t}\n\tdefer f.Close()\n\n\tinfo, err := f.Stat()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", false\n\t}\n\tsize := info.Size()\n\tif size == 0 {\n\t\treturn \"\", true\n\t}\n\n\tstart := int64(0)\n\ttruncated := false\n\tif size > jdiRunLogTailBytes {\n\t\tstart = size - jdiRunLogTailBytes\n\t\ttruncated = true\n\t}\n\tif _, err := f.Seek(start, io.SeekStart); err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", false\n\t}\n\tdata, err := io.ReadAll(f)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", false\n\t}\n\n\ttext := string(data)\n\tif truncated {\n\t\t// Drop a possibly-partial first line so the tail starts cleanly at\n\t\t// an invocation boundary rather than mid-line.\n\t\tif idx := strings.IndexByte(text, '\\n'); idx >= 0 {\n\t\t\ttext = text[idx+1:]\n\t\t}\n\t\ttext = \"… (log truncated — showing the most recent portion)\\n\\n\" + text\n\t}\n\treturn text, true\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":255,"totalLines":255,"truncated":false}},"title":"src/internal/job/jdistatus.go","time":{"start":1787919598368,"end":1787919598372}},"id":"prt_0485002910019bhSi8V8BtjXnr","sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","messageID":"msg_0484ffc03001UObwaP6Ap88UZ0"}}
