{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787919864469,"sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ba68a0f7896941599d336221","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/draft-brief-capture-agent-sessions.md"},"output":"<path>/workspace/draft-brief-capture-agent-sessions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Brief: capture non-interactive agent sessions\n2: \n3: status: open\n4: type: feature\n5: id: <filled by mg job>\n6: branch: <filled by mg job>\n7: date: 2026-08-27\n8: author: Leander Muskalla\n9: \n10: ## What\n11: \n12: Capture the full output of every non-interactive agent invocation (`mg jdi` /\n13: `--print` runs) and persist it as a session log in the job's own folder, so\n14: what an agent *did* during an unattended run survives — instead of only its\n15: final answer.\n16: \n17: Concretely:\n18: \n19: - In the `mg jdi` run loop (`src/cmd/mg/jdi.go`, where the invocation's full\n20:   captured stdout already sits in memory), append that raw output to a session\n21:   log in the job's folder: `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`, one section\n22:   header per invocation (agent, attempt, timestamp) — the same sectioned shape\n23:   `run.log` already uses.\n24: - The session log is committed by the normal host-side sweep-commit, so it\n25:   rides the job's branch and is carried into the archive automatically by\n26:   `mg done`'s archive move. `mg delete` removes it with the job folder.\n27: - Switch the claude `--print` path (`scripts/entrypoint.sh`, the claude-code\n28:   branch) from `--output-format json` to `--output-format stream-json`, so\n29:   claude-pro runs emit step-level events (tool calls, intermediate messages)\n30:   instead of only the final result object.\n31: - Add a third parser branch in `internal/orchestrate/signal.go` for Claude's\n32:   stream-json shape: `ResultText` takes the final result event's text;\n33:   `DetectSignal` scans only assistant text events, never tool output. Keep the\n34:   existing single-result parse as a defensive fallback (stream-json has been\n35:   more version-volatile than the stable result shape).\n36: \n37: ## Why\n38: \n39: In interactive mode the human sees everything the agent does. In unattended\n40: mode (`mg jdi`) that information is thrown away — the run log keeps only each\n41: invocation's final answer, not the blow-by-blow. There is no way to look back\n42: into a session. The goal: capture the whole run now, worry about rendering it\n43: somewhere later.\n44: \n45: ## Out of scope\n46: \n47: - Rendering/tailing the session log anywhere (TUI log tab stays on `run.log`;\n48:   a tailer is a separate, later job).\n49: - Normalizing the raw event stream into a structured format.\n50: - Per-step cost surfacing (the raw capture may contain tokens/cost — that is\n51:   fine, but no UI for it).\n52: - Any change to `mg done` / `mg delete` behavior — the archive move carries\n53:   the session log for free.\n54: - Streaming/incremental reads during a run — buffered post-hoc persistence is\n55:   the v1 shape.\n56: \n57: ## Notes\n58: \n59: - The raw output is already fully captured in memory today at `jdi.go` (the\n60:   `out` buffer from `runner.Run`), then discarded after `logInvocation` /\n61:   `DetectSignal`. This job is mostly a persistence change plus the claude\n62:   format switch.\n63: - OpenCode profiles already emit the full step-level event stream\n64:   (`step_start` / `tool_use` / `text` / `step_finish` JSONL); claude-pro does\n65:   not until the format switch lands. After this job, both capture the session.\n66: - `run.log` (the sidecar summary, human-readable final prose) stays as-is —\n67:   it is the summary; `session.log` is the raw truth. They do not replace each\n68:   other.\n69: - Stream-json version volatility is the main reliability risk: the fallback to\n70:   the existing result-parse must cover the case where a future claude version\n71:   changes or drops the format.\n\n(End of file - total 71 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Brief: capture non-interactive agent sessions\n\nstatus: open\ntype: feature\nid: <filled by mg job>\nbranch: <filled by mg job>\ndate: 2026-08-27\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nCapture the full output of every non-interactive agent invocation (`mg jdi` /\n`--print` runs) and persist it as a session log in the job's own folder, so\nwhat an agent *did* during an unattended run survives — instead of only its\nfinal answer.\n\nConcretely:\n\n- In the `mg jdi` run loop (`src/cmd/mg/jdi.go`, where the invocation's full\n  captured stdout already sits in memory), append that raw output to a session","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/draft-brief-capture-agent-sessions.md","text":"# Brief: capture non-interactive agent sessions\n\nstatus: open\ntype: feature\nid: <filled by mg job>\nbranch: <filled by mg job>\ndate: 2026-08-27\nauthor: Leander Muskalla\n\n## What\n\nCapture the full output of every non-interactive agent invocation (`mg jdi` /\n`--print` runs) and persist it as a session log in the job's own folder, so\nwhat an agent *did* during an unattended run survives — instead of only its\nfinal answer.\n\nConcretely:\n\n- In the `mg jdi` run loop (`src/cmd/mg/jdi.go`, where the invocation's full\n  captured stdout already sits in memory), append that raw output to a session\n  log in the job's folder: `docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`, one section\n  header per invocation (agent, attempt, timestamp) — the same sectioned shape\n  `run.log` already uses.\n- The session log is committed by the normal host-side sweep-commit, so it\n  rides the job's branch and is carried into the archive automatically by\n  `mg done`'s archive move. `mg delete` removes it with the job folder.\n- Switch the claude `--print` path (`scripts/entrypoint.sh`, the claude-code\n  branch) from `--output-format json` to `--output-format stream-json`, so\n  claude-pro runs emit step-level events (tool calls, intermediate messages)\n  instead of only the final result object.\n- Add a third parser branch in `internal/orchestrate/signal.go` for Claude's\n  stream-json shape: `ResultText` takes the final result event's text;\n  `DetectSignal` scans only assistant text events, never tool output. Keep the\n  existing single-result parse as a defensive fallback (stream-json has been\n  more version-volatile than the stable result shape).\n\n## Why\n\nIn interactive mode the human sees everything the agent does. In unattended\nmode (`mg jdi`) that information is thrown away — the run log keeps only each\ninvocation's final answer, not the blow-by-blow. There is no way to look back\ninto a session. The goal: capture the whole run now, worry about rendering it\nsomewhere later.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- Rendering/tailing the session log anywhere (TUI log tab stays on `run.log`;\n  a tailer is a separate, later job).\n- Normalizing the raw event stream into a structured format.\n- Per-step cost surfacing (the raw capture may contain tokens/cost — that is\n  fine, but no UI for it).\n- Any change to `mg done` / `mg delete` behavior — the archive move carries\n  the session log for free.\n- Streaming/incremental reads during a run — buffered post-hoc persistence is\n  the v1 shape.\n\n## Notes\n\n- The raw output is already fully captured in memory today at `jdi.go` (the\n  `out` buffer from `runner.Run`), then discarded after `logInvocation` /\n  `DetectSignal`. This job is mostly a persistence change plus the claude\n  format switch.\n- OpenCode profiles already emit the full step-level event stream\n  (`step_start` / `tool_use` / `text` / `step_finish` JSONL); claude-pro does\n  not until the format switch lands. After this job, both capture the session.\n- `run.log` (the sidecar summary, human-readable final prose) stays as-is —\n  it is the summary; `session.log` is the raw truth. They do not replace each\n  other.\n- Stream-json version volatility is the main reliability risk: the fallback to\n  the existing result-parse must cover the case where a future claude version\n  changes or drops the format.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":71,"totalLines":71,"truncated":false}},"title":"draft-brief-capture-agent-sessions.md","time":{"start":1787919864463,"end":1787919864466}},"id":"prt_048541201001jIFURF0NUOVLDD","sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","messageID":"msg_04853ac26001m2AgiEV1vvl1KO"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787919864977,"sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_db81218cf10c43a1bde40223","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/ROADMAP.md","limit":50,"offset":90},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/ROADMAP.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n90: The autonomy story's completion: \"set it on a task, it'll handle everything\"\n91: (the `vu33rn` Why). The hard 80% already exists — detached TUI-launched runs,\n92: ntfy notifications, status sidecars, the `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT` marker. Missing:\n93: a way to queue several jobs (`mg jdi --all`, or an ordered queue), a\n94: non-terminal trigger, and an *away digest* — \"here's what ran, here's what\n95: finished, here's what needs a human\" — surfaced through the notification\n96: channel already built. The old \"attended terminal\" objection is mostly\n97: obsoleted by the machinery built since.\n98: \n99: ### 5. Event-streaming subsystem — designed against a real consumer, now in scope\n100: \n101: **Status: PARTIALLY addressed** — the writer side landed in\n102: `health_capture-non-interactive-agent-sessions`: every non-interactive\n103: invocation's raw step-level output is persisted to the job's own\n104: `session.log` (and claude-pro's `--print` path now emits the full\n105: stream-json event stream), superseding the README's honest \"final answer\n106: only\" limitation. The item's *reader side in the TUI* — a richer step-level\n107: view off that capture — remains open (the TUI log tab stays on `run.log`\n108: for now; rendering/tailing `session.log` is a separate, later job).\n109: \n110: The polling model tells you the *stage*, not what the agent is *doing*. The\n111: old fear (\"wire-format commitment\") is answered by building it against a\n112: concrete need rather than as an abstraction: the first consumer in scope is\n113: the richer step-level `run.log` (the old \"richer logging\" backlog item),\n114: replacing the README's honest \"final answer only\" limitation. Design the\n115: writer side in the agent invocation path and the reader side in the TUI for\n116: *that* consumer; the format earns its keep before anything else attaches to\n117: it. One sizing constraint to pin before the writer design: the CLIs are\n118: asymmetric in what they emit. OpenCode's `run --format json` already streams\n119: fine-grained events (`step_start` / `tool_use` / `text` / `step_finish` with\n120: per-step tokens). Claude Code's `--print` today returns a single final-result\n121: object (`--output-format json`); it *can* stream (`--output-format\n122: stream-json`: per-message events, cost only as a per-run total on the result\n123: event) but manigot does not use it yet, and that format is more version-volatile\n124: than the stable result shape. So the writer needs either a third parser branch\n125: (Claude stream-json) or a coarser event model under `claude-pro`.\n126: \n127: ### 6. In-TUI embedded terminal — biggest bet, last, smallest slice first, now in scope\n128: \n129: The window-chaos fix, and the largest commitment on the list (PTY in Bubble\n130: Tea). Ordered last deliberately: its first useful slice — a live \"what is the\n131: running agent doing right now\" view in the detail view — can ride on the event\n132: stream from item 5 instead of inventing visibility from scratch. Full\n133: interactive embedding (typing to the agent inside the TUI) is a separate,\n134: later slice. Items 5 and 6 must not collapse into one job; the event system\n135: must not be designed by the terminal's requirements.\n136: \n137: ## Concerns\n138: \n139: - **Four big bets in one roadmap is fine; four big bets in one quarter is\n\n(Showing lines 90-139 of 163. Use offset=140 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"The autonomy story's completion: \"set it on a task, it'll handle everything\"\n(the `vu33rn` Why). The hard 80% already exists — detached TUI-launched runs,\nntfy notifications, status sidecars, the `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT` marker. Missing:\na way to queue several jobs (`mg jdi --all`, or an ordered queue), a\nnon-terminal trigger, and an *away digest* — \"here's what ran, here's what\nfinished, here's what needs a human\" — surfaced through the notification\nchannel already built. The old \"attended terminal\" objection is mostly\nobsoleted by the machinery built since.\n\n### 5. Event-streaming subsystem — designed against a real consumer, now in scope\n\n**Status: PARTIALLY addressed** — the writer side landed in\n`health_capture-non-interactive-agent-sessions`: every non-interactive\ninvocation's raw step-level output is persisted to the job's own\n`session.log` (and claude-pro's `--print` path now emits the full\nstream-json event stream), superseding the README's honest \"final answer\nonly\" limitation. The item's *reader side in the TUI* — a richer step-level\nview off that capture — remains open (the TUI log tab stays on `run.log`\nfor now; rendering/tailing `session.log` is a separate, later job).\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/ROADMAP.md","text":"The autonomy story's completion: \"set it on a task, it'll handle everything\"\n(the `vu33rn` Why). The hard 80% already exists — detached TUI-launched runs,\nntfy notifications, status sidecars, the `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT` marker. Missing:\na way to queue several jobs (`mg jdi --all`, or an ordered queue), a\nnon-terminal trigger, and an *away digest* — \"here's what ran, here's what\nfinished, here's what needs a human\" — surfaced through the notification\nchannel already built. The old \"attended terminal\" objection is mostly\nobsoleted by the machinery built since.\n\n### 5. Event-streaming subsystem — designed against a real consumer, now in scope\n\n**Status: PARTIALLY addressed** — the writer side landed in\n`health_capture-non-interactive-agent-sessions`: every non-interactive\ninvocation's raw step-level output is persisted to the job's own\n`session.log` (and claude-pro's `--print` path now emits the full\nstream-json event stream), superseding the README's honest \"final answer\nonly\" limitation. The item's *reader side in the TUI* — a richer step-level\nview off that capture — remains open (the TUI log tab stays on `run.log`\nfor now; rendering/tailing `session.log` is a separate, later job).\n\nThe polling model tells you the *stage*, not what the agent is *doing*. The\nold fear (\"wire-format commitment\") is answered by building it against a\nconcrete need rather than as an abstraction: the first consumer in scope is\nthe richer step-level `run.log` (the old \"richer logging\" backlog item),\nreplacing the README's honest \"final answer only\" limitation. Design the\nwriter side in the agent invocation path and the reader side in the TUI for\n*that* consumer; the format earns its keep before anything else attaches to\nit. One sizing constraint to pin before the writer design: the CLIs are\nasymmetric in what they emit. OpenCode's `run --format json` already streams\nfine-grained events (`step_start` / `tool_use` / `text` / `step_finish` with\nper-step tokens). Claude Code's `--print` today returns a single final-result\nobject (`--output-format json`); it *can* stream (`--output-format\nstream-json`: per-message events, cost only as a per-run total on the result\nevent) but manigot does not use it yet, and that format is more version-volatile\nthan the stable result shape. So the writer needs either a third parser branch\n(Claude stream-json) or a coarser event model under `claude-pro`.\n\n### 6. In-TUI embedded terminal — biggest bet, last, smallest slice first, now in scope\n\nThe window-chaos fix, and the largest commitment on the list (PTY in Bubble\nTea). Ordered last deliberately: its first useful slice — a live \"what is the\nrunning agent doing right now\" view in the detail view — can ride on the event\nstream from item 5 instead of inventing visibility from scratch. Full\ninteractive embedding (typing to the agent inside the TUI) is a separate,\nlater slice. Items 5 and 6 must not collapse into one job; the event system\nmust not be designed by the terminal's requirements.\n\n## Concerns\n\n- **Four big bets in one roadmap is fine; four big bets in one quarter is","lineStart":90,"lineEnd":139,"totalLines":163,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/ROADMAP.md","time":{"start":1787919864971,"end":1787919864974}},"id":"prt_0485412b4001UOTIhJnwui04n8","sessionID":"ses_fb7b04d47ffelTPa5RFrjm6f8o","messageID":"msg_04853ac26001m2AgiEV1vvl1KO"}}
