{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1787920103359,"sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","part":{"id":"prt_04857b7bd001akAefagaXbKPSb","messageID":"msg_04857b100001PPHjV2ho8mOW1J","sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","snapshot":"","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787920104293,"sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_b7266e837dd64699a176ddaa","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/README.md","offset":855,"limit":60},"output":"<path>/workspace/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n855: ```\n856: \n857: The TUI's `j` keybinding (see below) passes its settings profile — the shared\n858: `MANIGOT_PROFILE` default — the same way `@name` agent launches already do;\n859: no separate configuration needed there.\n860: \n861: **Watching a run.** A direct `mg jdi --job <id>` run streams each agent's\n862: output to its own terminal as each invocation completes, and rings the\n863: terminal bell when it stops. Every invocation's raw step-level output — the\n864: whole blow-by-blow of tool calls and intermediate messages, not just the\n865: final answer — is also persisted to the job's own `session.log`\n866: (`docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`), so what an agent did during an\n867: unattended run survives and rides the job's branch into the archive\n868: (rendering/tailing it is a separate, later feature). A TUI-launched run (see\n869: below) has no terminal of its own at all — the list's status badge and the\n870: detail view's log tab are how you watch it there instead.\n871: \n872: **Getting notified.** When `mg jdi` runs unattended on a VPS, you don't want\n873: to watch the terminal for it to stop. Set `NTFY_TOPIC` in `manigot/.env`\n874: (`mg setup` walks you through the three keys) and it pushes an ntfy\n875: notification to your phone whenever a run stops: a success notification when\n876: it finishes, a high-priority attention one when it stops needing a human —\n877: and, at the next run's start, if it finds the previous run crashed or\n878: killed. `NTFY_URL` defaults to `https://ntfy.sh`; `NTFY_TOKEN` is optional\n879: (sent as a `Bearer` auth header). Notifications are opt-in: without\n880: `NTFY_TOPIC`, nothing is sent and behavior is unchanged.\n881: \n882: ---\n883: \n884: ## TUI\n885: \n886: manigot ships an optional terminal UI for browsing jobs and launching agents\n887: without remembering command syntax. It is **host-side**: it runs on your\n888: machine as `mg tui` (in-process — no separate binary), reads a project's\n889: `docs/jobs/`, and launches sessions through the same in-process session\n890: launcher the CLI uses. It does **not** run in the container and needs no\n891: credentials itself.\n892: \n893: Every open job lives in its own **git worktree** (see\n894: [Job workflow](#job-workflow)) — one worktree per job branch, created by\n895: `mg job` and removed by `mg done`/`mg delete` — so the job list is\n896: discovered straight from `git worktree list`: each worktree's\n897: `docs/jobs/` is read off its own disk, and the project root's own working\n898: tree stays on the base branch. There is no \"wrong branch checked out\" state\n899: to worry about: every action — editing a brief, launching an agent, marking\n900: done, deleting — targets the job's own worktree directly, so nothing is ever\n901: guarded on the currently checked-out branch.\n902: \n903: ### Supported platforms\n904: \n905: macOS and Linux. Firing an agent opens `mg --profile <profile> --agent <name>\n906: --job <id>` in a new terminal (`<profile>` from the settings screen — see\n907: below), picked in this order:\n908: \n909: 1. a **tmux** split pane in the TUI's own window, if the TUI is itself running\n910:    inside tmux (`$TMUX` set) — each new launch replaces the pane manigot opened\n911:    before, so at most one agent pane exists at a time. Inside tmux this always\n912:    wins, even when a **Terminal** is set (see below): the session opens in a\n913:    tmux pane, never in a separate window.\n914: 2. the **Terminal** from the settings screen (`s` from the job list), when\n\n(Showing lines 855-914 of 1217. Use offset=915 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"```\n\nThe TUI's `j` keybinding (see below) passes its settings profile — the shared\n`MANIGOT_PROFILE` default — the same way `@name` agent launches already do;\nno separate configuration needed there.\n\n**Watching a run.** A direct `mg jdi --job <id>` run streams each agent's\noutput to its own terminal as each invocation completes, and rings the\nterminal bell when it stops. Every invocation's raw step-level output — the\nwhole blow-by-blow of tool calls and intermediate messages, not just the\nfinal answer — is also persisted to the job's own `session.log`\n(`docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`), so what an agent did during an\nunattended run survives and rides the job's branch into the archive\n(rendering/tailing it is a separate, later feature). A TUI-launched run (see\nbelow) has no terminal of its own at all — the list's status badge and the\ndetail view's log tab are how you watch it there instead.\n\n**Getting notified.** When `mg jdi` runs unattended on a VPS, you don't want\nto watch the terminal for it to stop. Set `NTFY_TOPIC` in `manigot/.env`\n(`mg setup` walks you through the three keys) and it pushes an ntfy","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/README.md","text":"```\n\nThe TUI's `j` keybinding (see below) passes its settings profile — the shared\n`MANIGOT_PROFILE` default — the same way `@name` agent launches already do;\nno separate configuration needed there.\n\n**Watching a run.** A direct `mg jdi --job <id>` run streams each agent's\noutput to its own terminal as each invocation completes, and rings the\nterminal bell when it stops. Every invocation's raw step-level output — the\nwhole blow-by-blow of tool calls and intermediate messages, not just the\nfinal answer — is also persisted to the job's own `session.log`\n(`docs/jobs/<id>_<slug>/session.log`), so what an agent did during an\nunattended run survives and rides the job's branch into the archive\n(rendering/tailing it is a separate, later feature). A TUI-launched run (see\nbelow) has no terminal of its own at all — the list's status badge and the\ndetail view's log tab are how you watch it there instead.\n\n**Getting notified.** When `mg jdi` runs unattended on a VPS, you don't want\nto watch the terminal for it to stop. Set `NTFY_TOPIC` in `manigot/.env`\n(`mg setup` walks you through the three keys) and it pushes an ntfy\nnotification to your phone whenever a run stops: a success notification when\nit finishes, a high-priority attention one when it stops needing a human —\nand, at the next run's start, if it finds the previous run crashed or\nkilled. `NTFY_URL` defaults to `https://ntfy.sh`; `NTFY_TOKEN` is optional\n(sent as a `Bearer` auth header). Notifications are opt-in: without\n`NTFY_TOPIC`, nothing is sent and behavior is unchanged.\n\n---\n\n## TUI\n\nmanigot ships an optional terminal UI for browsing jobs and launching agents\nwithout remembering command syntax. It is **host-side**: it runs on your\nmachine as `mg tui` (in-process — no separate binary), reads a project's\n`docs/jobs/`, and launches sessions through the same in-process session\nlauncher the CLI uses. It does **not** run in the container and needs no\ncredentials itself.\n\nEvery open job lives in its own **git worktree** (see\n[Job workflow](#job-workflow)) — one worktree per job branch, created by\n`mg job` and removed by `mg done`/`mg delete` — so the job list is\ndiscovered straight from `git worktree list`: each worktree's\n`docs/jobs/` is read off its own disk, and the project root's own working\ntree stays on the base branch. There is no \"wrong branch checked out\" state\nto worry about: every action — editing a brief, launching an agent, marking\ndone, deleting — targets the job's own worktree directly, so nothing is ever\nguarded on the currently checked-out branch.\n\n### Supported platforms\n\nmacOS and Linux. Firing an agent opens `mg --profile <profile> --agent <name>\n--job <id>` in a new terminal (`<profile>` from the settings screen — see\nbelow), picked in this order:\n\n1. a **tmux** split pane in the TUI's own window, if the TUI is itself running\n   inside tmux (`$TMUX` set) — each new launch replaces the pane manigot opened\n   before, so at most one agent pane exists at a time. Inside tmux this always\n   wins, even when a **Terminal** is set (see below): the session opens in a\n   tmux pane, never in a separate window.\n2. the **Terminal** from the settings screen (`s` from the job list), when","lineStart":855,"lineEnd":914,"totalLines":1217,"truncated":true}},"title":"README.md","time":{"start":1787920104284,"end":1787920104291}},"id":"prt_04857b8ef001i6taTQrucg1v0M","sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","messageID":"msg_04857b100001PPHjV2ho8mOW1J"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787920104644,"sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_a051ddaa61c847798c376728","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/README.md","offset":975,"limit":70},"output":"<path>/workspace/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n975: |---|---|\n976: | `tab` / `1`-`6` | switch tab: brief · tasks · implementation · verdict · log · diff |\n977: | `pgup`/`pgdn`, `home`/`G` | scroll |\n978: | `p` `a` `d` `r` `s` | run the agent shown in the action bar (Owner, Analyst, Developer, Reviewer, Security — all five are always available, regardless of the job's stage) |\n979: | `e` | edit `brief.md` in `$EDITOR` (only on the brief tab — tasks/implementation/verdict/log/diff are agent-, mg jdi-, or TUI-computed) |\n980: | `D` | mark the job done — shows an in-TUI confirmation, then runs the in-process finish lifecycle (squash-merge + archive) |\n981: | `j` | run `mg jdi` against this job, detached in the background — no window is opened; watch it via the log tab or the list's status badge (see [Autonomous mode](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi) and [mg jdi status & log](#mg-jdi-status--log) below) |\n982: | `x` / `del` | permanently delete the job — shows an in-TUI confirmation (with a dirty-worktree warning when the job's worktree has uncommitted changes), then runs the in-process delete lifecycle. `x` exists because the physical Delete/Entf key's escape sequence isn't decoded consistently by every terminal — both trigger the same action |\n983: | `P` | push this job's branch to `origin` (`git push -u origin <branch>`) — a quick way to make it visible on another host via `git pull` |\n984: | `t` | open the job's branch diff in tig (`mg diff <id> --tig`) — spawns in a tmux split pane / new terminal like agent launches; only available when tig is installed on the host |\n985: | `l` | tail this job's `mg jdi` `run.log` live — spawns `tail -f` in a tmux split pane / new terminal like agent launches; only available once an `mg jdi` run has happened for this job (the same gate as the log tab's real content) |\n986: | `c` | commit all uncommitted changes in this job's worktree (`git add -A` + `git commit` with a `[<id>] chore: commit all` message) — a catch-all sweep for the files agents sometimes leave behind, so `D`'s clean-tree check isn't tripped |\n987: | `g` | open the git panel — pick one of the detail view's git commands for this job's worktree: commit all, push to origin, or merge default branch (see below) |\n988: | `ctrl+r` | refresh (the TUI also refreshes itself automatically every second) |\n989: | `esc` | back to list |\n990: \n991: The **log** tab shows `mg jdi`'s `run.log` for this job — one section per\n992: agent invocation, with a timestamp/agent/attempt header; the attempt number\n993: counts that agent's invocations within the run (per agent, not per run), so\n994: a bounce back to the developer shows that developer's second call as attempt\n995: 2. It reads\n996: \"_no mg jdi run has happened for this job yet_\" until the first `mg jdi` run\n997: against it; large files are tailed, not loaded in full. Never editable.\n998: \n999: The **diff** tab is the TUI's representation of `mg diff <id>`: it computes\n1000: what the job's branch changed relative to the project's base branch — the\n1001: same quick eyeball the CLI prints by default (`git log --oneline` followed by\n1002: `git diff --stat` over the three-dot range `<base>...<branch>`, per\n1003: [docs/GIT_DIFF.md](docs/GIT_DIFF.md)), so you can review the job's changes\n1004: in the TUI before marking it done with `D`. The base branch resolves exactly\n1005: as `mg diff` does — the project's configured `baseBranch`, falling back to\n1006: `origin/HEAD` → `main` — and the tab degrades instead of crashing: a job\n1007: with no branch (not a git worktree job) or a git error shows a plain-text\n1008: placeholder, and an undiverged branch reads \"No changes on `<branch>`\n1009: relative to `<base>`.\" It is recomputed on every refresh (the automatic\n1010: every-second refresh and `ctrl+r` alike), so newly committed changes appear\n1011: without a manual refresh. Never editable.\n1012: \n1013: Below the footer, the job view also shows a **git-log strip** — the same dim\n1014: `shortHash  subject  relTime  branch` lines the job list's recent-activity\n1015: strip renders at the bottom of the screen, scoped to just this job's own\n1016: branch (`git.BranchCommits`, the job-branch counterpart of the list's\n1017: cross-branch `git.RecentCommits`). It is refreshed when the job opens and on\n1018: every refresh (the automatic every-second refresh and `ctrl+r` alike), sized\n1019: like the list's strip (up to the settings'\n1020: recent-activity count, floor of one line), and disappears entirely for a\n1021: job with no branch or a project that isn't a git repo.\n1022: \n1023: The **git panel** (`g`) is a small modal listing the detail view's git\n1024: commands — **Commit all**, **Push to origin**, and **Merge default branch**\n1025: — one per row, moved through with `↑`/`↓` and run with `enter`\n1026: (`esc`/`q` cancels back to the detail view). The first two are the same\n1027: actions as the `c`/`P` accelerators; **Merge default branch** brings a job's\n1028: worktree up to speed before starting work: it runs `git merge --no-edit`\n1029: inside the job's own worktree, merging in the project's locally-resolved\n1030: base branch (the configured `baseBranch`, falling back to `origin/HEAD` →\n1031: `main` — the same local base the diff tab diffs against and `D` merges into).\n1032: It never fetches, so it merges whatever state that base branch is in\n1033: locally; a worktree that is already up to date is a successful no-op, and a\n1034: merge conflict leaves the tree in git's conflicted state for you to resolve\n1035: manually (the detail view's status line reports the conflict).\n1036: \n1037: `e` resolves the editor to run as: the settings screen's Editor field (see\n1038: below), if set; otherwise `$VISUAL`, then `$EDITOR`, then whichever of\n1039: `nano`/`vi` is found first on `PATH`.\n1040: \n1041: New-job form: type a title, `tab` to the type field and use `←`/`→` to pick\n1042: `feature`/`fix`/`chore`, `enter` to create, `esc` to cancel.\n1043: \n1044: ### Settings\n\n(Showing lines 975-1044 of 1217. Use offset=1045 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"|---|---|\n| `tab` / `1`-`6` | switch tab: brief · tasks · implementation · verdict · log · diff |\n| `pgup`/`pgdn`, `home`/`G` | scroll |\n| `p` `a` `d` `r` `s` | run the agent shown in the action bar (Owner, Analyst, Developer, Reviewer, Security — all five are always available, regardless of the job's stage) |\n| `e` | edit `brief.md` in `$EDITOR` (only on the brief tab — tasks/implementation/verdict/log/diff are agent-, mg jdi-, or TUI-computed) |\n| `D` | mark the job done — shows an in-TUI confirmation, then runs the in-process finish lifecycle (squash-merge + archive) |\n| `j` | run `mg jdi` against this job, detached in the background — no window is opened; watch it via the log tab or the list's status badge (see [Autonomous mode](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi) and [mg jdi status & log](#mg-jdi-status--log) below) |\n| `x` / `del` | permanently delete the job — shows an in-TUI confirmation (with a dirty-worktree warning when the job's worktree has uncommitted changes), then runs the in-process delete lifecycle. `x` exists because the physical Delete/Entf key's escape sequence isn't decoded consistently by every terminal — both trigger the same action |\n| `P` | push this job's branch to `origin` (`git push -u origin <branch>`) — a quick way to make it visible on another host via `git pull` |\n| `t` | open the job's branch diff in tig (`mg diff <id> --tig`) — spawns in a tmux split pane / new terminal like agent launches; only available when tig is installed on the host |\n| `l` | tail this job's `mg jdi` `run.log` live — spawns `tai