{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787920106379,"sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_edd6e2b44cf845db910dfb4d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/README.md","limit":50,"offset":1100},"output":"<path>/workspace/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1100: to the in-process finish lifecycle, which squash-merges the job branch into\n1101: the default branch, archives the job directory under `docs/jobs/archive/`,\n1102: and sets `status: done`. This is available from any stage too.\n1103: \n1104: ### mg jdi status & log\n1105: \n1106: Press `j` in the list (on the selected job) or in the detail view to start\n1107: [`mg jdi`](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi) against that job. Unlike the agent-launch\n1108: keys above, this opens **no\n1109: terminal window at all** — `mg jdi` has no interactive session for a human\n1110: or a subprocess to attach to, so it runs fully detached in the background.\n1111: Two places show you what it's doing instead, both polled the same\n1112: refresh-triggered way as everything else in the TUI (the automatic\n1113: every-second refresh, pressing `ctrl+r`, returning to the list, etc. — no\n1114: separate live-streaming subsystem), plus a live tail you can open yourself.\n1115: The two narrow exceptions are self-terminating timer-driven redraws: the\n1116: status blink/expiry timer while a status is set, and one that runs *only\n1117: while a run is active*, driving the animated indicator below:\n1118: \n1119: - **List-row badge** — a `[running @<agent>]`, `[finished]`, or\n1120:   `[needs human]` tag next to a job's row.\n1121:   While a run is active the running badge is prefixed\n1122:   with an animated activity indicator — a small spinner\n1123:   (`⠋ [running @<agent>]`), the same idea as the one opencode shows in its\n1124:   bottom-left corner — so a watched run visibly indicates it's alive instead\n1125:   of sitting as a static tag that only changes on the next refresh. Shown\n1126:   only while there's something live or recent\n1127:   to report; a stale status left behind by a killed `mg jdi` process is\n1128:   never shown as if it were current.\n1129: - **Log tab** — see [Keybindings](#keybindings) above.\n1130: - **Live tail pane** — press `l` in the detail view (available once a\n1131:   `run.log` exists for the job) to spawn a live `tail -f` of the job's\n1132:   `run.log` in a tmux split pane / new terminal — the same sidecar the log\n1133:   tab reads, but updating without any refresh. Like the log tab, it updates\n1134:   per agent invocation (mg-jdi appends at invocation boundaries, not\n1135:   mid-invocation), and `tail -f` idles harmlessly once a run ends; end the\n1136:   pane with Ctrl+C like any tail.\n1137: \n1138: **Notification.** A direct `mg jdi --job <id>` run rings the terminal bell\n1139: itself when it stops (see [Autonomous mode](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi)). A\n1140: `j`-launched run has no terminal to ring into, so the TUI rings it instead,\n1141: on its own next poll, the first time it notices that job's status turn into\n1142: `finished` or `needs human` — once per fresh stop, not on every poll while\n1143: it stays stopped, and never for a job that was already stopped before this\n1144: TUI session started watching it.\n1145: \n1146: ---\n1147: \n1148: ## Listener\n1149: \n\n(Showing lines 1100-1149 of 1217. Use offset=1150 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"to the in-process finish lifecycle, which squash-merges the job branch into\nthe default branch, archives the job directory under `docs/jobs/archive/`,\nand sets `status: done`. This is available from any stage too.\n\n### mg jdi status & log\n\nPress `j` in the list (on the selected job) or in the detail view to start\n[`mg jdi`](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi) against that job. Unlike the agent-launch\nkeys above, this opens **no\nterminal window at all** — `mg jdi` has no interactive session for a human\nor a subprocess to attach to, so it runs fully detached in the background.\nTwo places show you what it's doing instead, both polled the same\nrefresh-triggered way as everything else in the TUI (the automatic\nevery-second refresh, pressing `ctrl+r`, returning to the list, etc. — no\nseparate live-streaming subsystem), plus a live tail you can open yourself.\nThe two narrow exceptions are self-terminating timer-driven redraws: the\nstatus blink/expiry timer while a status is set, and one that runs *only\nwhile a run is active*, driving the animated indicator below:\n\n- **List-row badge** — a `[running @<agent>]`, `[finished]`, or","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/README.md","text":"to the in-process finish lifecycle, which squash-merges the job branch into\nthe default branch, archives the job directory under `docs/jobs/archive/`,\nand sets `status: done`. This is available from any stage too.\n\n### mg jdi status & log\n\nPress `j` in the list (on the selected job) or in the detail view to start\n[`mg jdi`](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi) against that job. Unlike the agent-launch\nkeys above, this opens **no\nterminal window at all** — `mg jdi` has no interactive session for a human\nor a subprocess to attach to, so it runs fully detached in the background.\nTwo places show you what it's doing instead, both polled the same\nrefresh-triggered way as everything else in the TUI (the automatic\nevery-second refresh, pressing `ctrl+r`, returning to the list, etc. — no\nseparate live-streaming subsystem), plus a live tail you can open yourself.\nThe two narrow exceptions are self-terminating timer-driven redraws: the\nstatus blink/expiry timer while a status is set, and one that runs *only\nwhile a run is active*, driving the animated indicator below:\n\n- **List-row badge** — a `[running @<agent>]`, `[finished]`, or\n  `[needs human]` tag next to a job's row.\n  While a run is active the running badge is prefixed\n  with an animated activity indicator — a small spinner\n  (`⠋ [running @<agent>]`), the same idea as the one opencode shows in its\n  bottom-left corner — so a watched run visibly indicates it's alive instead\n  of sitting as a static tag that only changes on the next refresh. Shown\n  only while there's something live or recent\n  to report; a stale status left behind by a killed `mg jdi` process is\n  never shown as if it were current.\n- **Log tab** — see [Keybindings](#keybindings) above.\n- **Live tail pane** — press `l` in the detail view (available once a\n  `run.log` exists for the job) to spawn a live `tail -f` of the job's\n  `run.log` in a tmux split pane / new terminal — the same sidecar the log\n  tab reads, but updating without any refresh. Like the log tab, it updates\n  per agent invocation (mg-jdi appends at invocation boundaries, not\n  mid-invocation), and `tail -f` idles harmlessly once a run ends; end the\n  pane with Ctrl+C like any tail.\n\n**Notification.** A direct `mg jdi --job <id>` run rings the terminal bell\nitself when it stops (see [Autonomous mode](#autonomous-mode-mg-jdi)). A\n`j`-launched run has no terminal to ring into, so the TUI rings it instead,\non its own next poll, the first time it notices that job's status turn into\n`finished` or `needs human` — once per fresh stop, not on every poll while\nit stays stopped, and never for a job that was already stopped before this\nTUI session started watching it.\n\n---\n\n## Listener\n","lineStart":1100,"lineEnd":1149,"totalLines":1217,"truncated":true}},"title":"README.md","time":{"start":1787920106374,"end":1787920106378}},"id":"prt_04857c25e001ywRTPjfvUzRmie","sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","messageID":"msg_04857bd100015suz47J3dr06qH"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1787920106657,"sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_424f285211494999a9e866ac","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/docs/ROADMAP.md","limit":80,"offset":60},"output":"<path>/workspace/docs/ROADMAP.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n60: \n61: `mg delete` and `mg done` now both remove the job's `.manigot/jdi-status/<job>/`\n62: sidecar (status + run.log), as does orphaned-worktree removal — the\n63: keep-vs-remove decision for `mg done` is **remove**: the archive keeps the\n64: job's docs, mg-jdi never runs against an archived job, and the sidecar would\n65: otherwise be dead weight forever. Best-effort (a removal failure warns, never\n66: aborts the already-succeeded delete/finish). This repo's own stale sidecar\n67: dirs were cleaned up as part of the fix.\n68: \n69: ### 2. `mg doctor` health check (small, agreed)\n70: \n71: One command that verifies the chain in one place: image present, docker daemon\n72: up, profiles ready, git repo sane, worktree integrity. `mg setup --check`\n73: covers credentials only. Given the project's history of \"jdi does not work\"\n74: jobs, this pre-empts the silent-failure class of problem instead of fixing it\n75: after the fact.\n76: \n77: ### 3. Configurable project toolchains (Docker images) — reframed, now in scope\n78: \n79: The old rejection (\"need isn't evidenced\") was aimed at custom images per\n80: project. The real need is narrower and real: **an agent on a Node/Python/\n81: whatever project can't run that project's own build or test commands** because\n82: the single image doesn't carry the toolchain — an agent that can't verify its\n83: own work. Smallest useful version: keep the single base image as the default,\n84: let a project declare additions (extra packages/toolchains layered at session\n85: start) via a documented, `mg init`-era config. No from-scratch images, no\n86: per-project fork of the isolation story.\n87: \n88: ### 4. Headless / cron execution — the VPS promise, now in scope\n89: \n90: The autonomy story's completion: \"set it on a task, it'll handle everything\"\n91: (the `vu33rn` Why). The hard 80% already exists — detached TUI-launched runs,\n92: ntfy notifications, status sidecars, the `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT` marker. Missing:\n93: a way to queue several jobs (`mg jdi --all`, or an ordered queue), a\n94: non-terminal trigger, and an *away digest* — \"here's what ran, here's what\n95: finished, here's what needs a human\" — surfaced through the notification\n96: channel already built. The old \"attended terminal\" objection is mostly\n97: obsoleted by the machinery built since.\n98: \n99: ### 5. Event-streaming subsystem — designed against a real consumer, now in scope\n100: \n101: **Status: PARTIALLY addressed** — the writer side landed in\n102: `health_capture-non-interactive-agent-sessions`: every non-interactive\n103: invocation's raw step-level output is persisted to the job's own\n104: `session.log` (and claude-pro's `--print` path now emits the full\n105: stream-json event stream), superseding the README's honest \"final answer\n106: only\" limitation. The item's *reader side in the TUI* — a richer step-level\n107: view off that capture — remains open (the TUI log tab stays on `run.log`\n108: for now; rendering/tailing `session.log` is a separate, later job).\n109: \n110: The polling model tells you the *stage*, not what the agent is *doing*. The\n111: old fear (\"wire-format commitment\") is answered by building it against a\n112: concrete need rather than as an abstraction: the first consumer in scope is\n113: the richer step-level `run.log` (the old \"richer logging\" backlog item),\n114: replacing the README's honest \"final answer only\" limitation. Design the\n115: writer side in the agent invocation path and the reader side in the TUI for\n116: *that* consumer; the format earns its keep before anything else attaches to\n117: it. One sizing constraint to pin before the writer design: the CLIs are\n118: asymmetric in what they emit. OpenCode's `run --format json` already streams\n119: fine-grained events (`step_start` / `tool_use` / `text` / `step_finish` with\n120: per-step tokens). Claude Code's `--print` today returns a single final-result\n121: object (`--output-format json`); it *can* stream (`--output-format\n122: stream-json`: per-message events, cost only as a per-run total on the result\n123: event) but manigot does not use it yet, and that format is more version-volatile\n124: than the stable result shape. So the writer needs either a third parser branch\n125: (Claude stream-json) or a coarser event model under `claude-pro`.\n126: \n127: ### 6. In-TUI embedded terminal — biggest bet, last, smallest slice first, now in scope\n128: \n129: The window-chaos fix, and the largest commitment on the list (PTY in Bubble\n130: Tea). Ordered last deliberately: its first useful slice — a live \"what is the\n131: running agent doing right now\" view in the detail view — can ride on the event\n132: stream from item 5 instead of inventing visibility from scratch. Full\n133: interactive embedding (typing to the agent inside the TUI) is a separate,\n134: later slice. Items 5 and 6 must not collapse into one job; the event system\n135: must not be designed by the terminal's requirements.\n136: \n137: ## Concerns\n138: \n139: - **Four big bets in one roadmap is fine; four big bets in one quarter is\n\n(Showing lines 60-139 of 163. Use offset=140 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\n`mg delete` and `mg done` now both remove the job's `.manigot/jdi-status/<job>/`\nsidecar (status + run.log), as does orphaned-worktree removal — the\nkeep-vs-remove decision for `mg done` is **remove**: the archive keeps the\njob's docs, mg-jdi never runs against an archived job, and the sidecar would\notherwise be dead weight forever. Best-effort (a removal failure warns, never\naborts the already-succeeded delete/finish). This repo's own stale sidecar\ndirs were cleaned up as part of the fix.\n\n### 2. `mg doctor` health check (small, agreed)\n\nOne command that verifies the chain in one place: image present, docker daemon\nup, profiles ready, git repo sane, worktree integrity. `mg setup --check`\ncovers credentials only. Given the project's history of \"jdi does not work\"\njobs, this pre-empts the silent-failure class of problem instead of fixing it\nafter the fact.\n\n### 3. Configurable project toolchains (Docker images) — reframed, now in scope\n\nThe old rejection (\"need isn't evidenced\") was aimed at custom images per","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/workspace/docs/ROADMAP.md","text":"\n`mg delete` and `mg done` now both remove the job's `.manigot/jdi-status/<job>/`\nsidecar (status + run.log), as does orphaned-worktree removal — the\nkeep-vs-remove decision for `mg done` is **remove**: the archive keeps the\njob's docs, mg-jdi never runs against an archived job, and the sidecar would\notherwise be dead weight forever. Best-effort (a removal failure warns, never\naborts the already-succeeded delete/finish). This repo's own stale sidecar\ndirs were cleaned up as part of the fix.\n\n### 2. `mg doctor` health check (small, agreed)\n\nOne command that verifies the chain in one place: image present, docker daemon\nup, profiles ready, git repo sane, worktree integrity. `mg setup --check`\ncovers credentials only. Given the project's history of \"jdi does not work\"\njobs, this pre-empts the silent-failure class of problem instead of fixing it\nafter the fact.\n\n### 3. Configurable project toolchains (Docker images) — reframed, now in scope\n\nThe old rejection (\"need isn't evidenced\") was aimed at custom images per\nproject. The real need is narrower and real: **an agent on a Node/Python/\nwhatever project can't run that project's own build or test commands** because\nthe single image doesn't carry the toolchain — an agent that can't verify its\nown work. Smallest useful version: keep the single base image as the default,\nlet a project declare additions (extra packages/toolchains layered at session\nstart) via a documented, `mg init`-era config. No from-scratch images, no\nper-project fork of the isolation story.\n\n### 4. Headless / cron execution — the VPS promise, now in scope\n\nThe autonomy story's completion: \"set it on a task, it'll handle everything\"\n(the `vu33rn` Why). The hard 80% already exists — detached TUI-launched runs,\nntfy notifications, status sidecars, the `NEEDS-HUMAN-INPUT` marker. Missing:\na way to queue several jobs (`mg jdi --all`, or an ordered queue), a\nnon-terminal trigger, and an *away digest* — \"here's what ran, here's what\nfinished, here's what needs a human\" — surfaced through the notification\nchannel already built. The old \"attended terminal\" objection is mostly\nobsoleted by the machinery built since.\n\n### 5. Event-streaming subsystem — designed against a real consumer, now in scope\n\n**Status: PARTIALLY addressed** — the writer side landed in\n`health_capture-non-interactive-agent-sessions`: every non-interactive\ninvocation's raw step-level output is persisted to the job's own\n`session.log` (and claude-pro's `--print` path now emits the full\nstream-json event stream), superseding the README's honest \"final answer\nonly\" limitation. The item's *reader side in the TUI* — a richer step-level\nview off that capture — remains open (the TUI log tab stays on `run.log`\nfor now; rendering/tailing `session.log` is a separate, later job).\n\nThe polling model tells you the *stage*, not what the agent is *doing*. The\nold fear (\"wire-format commitment\") is answered by building it against a\nconcrete need rather than as an abstraction: the first consumer in scope is\nthe richer step-level `run.log` (the old \"richer logging\" backlog item),\nreplacing the README's honest \"final answer only\" limitation. Design the\nwriter side in the agent invocation path and the reader side in the TUI for\n*that* consumer; the format earns its keep before anything else attaches to\nit. One sizing constraint to pin before the writer design: the CLIs are\nasymmetric in what they emit. OpenCode's `run --format json` already streams\nfine-grained events (`step_start` / `tool_use` / `text` / `step_finish` with\nper-step tokens). Claude Code's `--print` today returns a single final-result\nobject (`--output-format json`); it *can* stream (`--output-format\nstream-json`: per-message events, cost only as a per-run total on the result\nevent) but manigot does not use it yet, and that format is more version-volatile\nthan the stable result shape. So the writer needs either a third parser branch\n(Claude stream-json) or a coarser event model under `claude-pro`.\n\n### 6. In-TUI embedded terminal — biggest bet, last, smallest slice first, now in scope\n\nThe window-chaos fix, and the largest commitment on the list (PTY in Bubble\nTea). Ordered last deliberately: its first useful slice — a live \"what is the\nrunning agent doing right now\" view in the detail view — can ride on the event\nstream from item 5 instead of inventing visibility from scratch. Full\ninteractive embedding (typing to the agent inside the TUI) is a separate,\nlater slice. Items 5 and 6 must not collapse into one job; the event system\nmust not be designed by the terminal's requirements.\n\n## Concerns\n\n- **Four big bets in one roadmap is fine; four big bets in one quarter is","lineStart":60,"lineEnd":139,"totalLines":163,"truncated":true}},"title":"docs/ROADMAP.md","time":{"start":1787920106653,"end":1787920106656}},"id":"prt_04857c3970016GupglIDPtTJKW","sessionID":"ses_fb7a913aeffe0rISXrjcCChio6","messageID":"msg_04857bd100015suz47J3dr06qH"}}
